1
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:07,400
So last lecture, Percy covered some

2
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:11,720
of the underlying mechanics, let's call it, of parallelism.

3
00:00:11,720 --> 00:00:13,720
Today, I'm going to do my favorite thing of talk

4
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:17,220
about all sorts of things you can know, like knowledge,

5
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:20,960
details, and trivia about how modern parallelism for language

6
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:25,265
model training works.

7
00:00:25,265 --> 00:00:26,640
And what we're going to try to do

8
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:30,520
is really get to grips with the many, many complexities that

9
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:32,439
are involved when you are having to train

10
00:00:32,439 --> 00:00:35,120
huge models on huge clusters.

11
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:36,520
It's going to turn out that there

12
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:39,240
are many different parallelization strategies

13
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:41,720
that you can use at once.

14
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:44,040
At the end, we will get to a slide where I say 4D

15
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:46,520
parallelism, because there's four different things that you

16
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:47,540
can do at once.

17
00:00:47,540 --> 00:00:50,080
Actually, even more that I'll talk about.

18
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:52,240
And it turns out that to parallelize at the largest

19
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:56,720
scale, you'll need to use all of them or most of them.

20
00:00:56,720 --> 00:00:58,920
And we'll discuss why.

21
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:01,560
Part of your assignment will be figuring out,

22
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:05,500
given a particular network topology and a particular model,

23
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:08,558
what is the optimal parallelization strategy for that?

24
00:01:08,558 --> 00:01:10,600
And then finally, I'll walk through some examples

25
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:12,800
of recent large scale training runs

26
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:15,480
with details that are published and show

27
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:18,260
you this stuff in action.

28
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:22,460
OK, so the very first part, I'll go through somewhat quickly.

29
00:01:22,460 --> 00:01:25,600
It's partially a review of basic collectives, basic networking

30
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:27,560
primitives, right.

31
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:30,880
But the reason why we need all of this parallelism stuff

32
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:33,520
is because there's going to be two bottlenecks that we'll

33
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:36,740
solve by going to multiple machines and multiple GPUs.

34
00:01:36,740 --> 00:01:38,100
So one of it is compute.

35
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:41,640
We need more compute than we can bring to bear on a single chip.

36
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:45,080
And the way we solve that is just to have many, many machines

37
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:46,443
and link them up together.

38
00:01:46,443 --> 00:01:48,360
Right, the fastest supercomputers in the world

39
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:52,160
have exaflops of compute, whereas a single GPU

40
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:55,080
is nowhere near that number.

41
00:01:55,080 --> 00:01:58,600
The other reason why we need to continue to scale in parallel

42
00:01:58,600 --> 00:01:59,800
is because of memory, right.

43
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:00,780
Models are big.

44
00:02:00,780 --> 00:02:02,500
We can't fit them all into one GPU,

45
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:06,532
so we need to shard them into smaller pieces somehow.

46
00:02:06,532 --> 00:02:08,199
So this is going to lead to complexities

47
00:02:08,199 --> 00:02:11,360
of having many GPUs across many different machines.

48
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:13,480
And an important conceptual object

49
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:16,840
that we will deal with throughout our lecture today

50
00:02:16,840 --> 00:02:19,920
is the difference between intranode parallelism,

51
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:21,993
where your connections are very fast,

52
00:02:21,993 --> 00:02:24,160
and so you can do things that are very communication

53
00:02:24,160 --> 00:02:25,240
expensive.

54
00:02:25,240 --> 00:02:28,422
And you're going to have slower internode communication.

55
00:02:28,422 --> 00:02:29,880
And because these are slow, we will

56
00:02:29,880 --> 00:02:33,840
have to use processes that respect the communication

57
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:35,940
constraints of our channel more, right.

58
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:38,920
So we're going to have to do both of those.

59
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:42,063
And remember all of the things that we will do today.

60
00:02:42,063 --> 00:02:44,480
We're not actually going to be sending packets or anything

61
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:44,860
like that.

62
00:02:44,860 --> 00:02:46,320
We're going to be dealing with this

63
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:49,440
at the level of collective communication primitives.

64
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:51,360
Right, so whenever we do accounting

65
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:53,180
for how much communication is done,

66
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:55,880
I might say, OK, we're going to have to do all-reduce

67
00:02:55,880 --> 00:02:58,240
or we're going to have to do allgather to implement

68
00:02:58,240 --> 00:02:59,500
some of these operations.

69
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:03,338
So in order to implement some of these efficiently,

70
00:03:03,338 --> 00:03:05,880
you're going to have to go all the way to the hardware level.

71
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:08,140
But our discussion today is going to be algorithmic.

72
00:03:08,140 --> 00:03:10,600
And so all of the networking primitives I'll talk about

73
00:03:10,600 --> 00:03:14,037
is going to be at this level of a collective.

74
00:03:14,037 --> 00:03:15,620
And remember, Percy talked about this,

75
00:03:15,620 --> 00:03:17,400
but this is important for--

76
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:19,400
very important for one part of the talk, which

77
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:21,040
is going to be that the equivalence

78
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:24,220
between all-reduce and reduce-scatter plus all-gather.

79
00:03:24,220 --> 00:03:27,260
Right, so there's going to be one algorithm where on one hand,

80
00:03:27,260 --> 00:03:29,140
you do an all-reduce, and on the other hand,

81
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:31,180
You do two of these different steps.

82
00:03:31,180 --> 00:03:35,240
And the fact that these two have the same cost, in some sense,

83
00:03:35,240 --> 00:03:38,140
means that we can do the algorithm on the right for free.

84
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:39,540
We'll see that again later.

85
00:03:39,540 --> 00:03:41,640
This is just a quick refresher, just

86
00:03:41,640 --> 00:03:44,720
to make sure you remember what Percy said.

87
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:48,810
OK, so that's it for the really basic review.

88
00:03:48,810 --> 00:03:50,560
The other thing I want to touch on briefly

89
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:52,420
before we get into the algorithms,

90
00:03:52,420 --> 00:03:55,550
which are going to be hardware and substrate agnostic,

91
00:03:55,550 --> 00:03:57,870
is the difference-- oh, sorry, yes?

92
00:03:57,870 --> 00:04:00,590
Is there any particular reason why you are saying that old

93
00:04:00,590 --> 00:04:03,710
[INAUDIBLE] reduce scatter and--

94
00:04:03,710 --> 00:04:06,230
OK, the question was, is there any reason why I'm talking

95
00:04:06,230 --> 00:04:07,410
about this specifically?

96
00:04:07,410 --> 00:04:10,570
We will--[INAUDIBLE]

97
00:04:11,070 --> 00:04:13,090
Yes, yes, there are other decompositions.

98
00:04:13,090 --> 00:04:15,270
This one in particular will be useful for an algorithm, which

99
00:04:15,270 --> 00:04:17,329
is why I'm emphasizing this particular decomposition.

100
00:04:17,329 --> 00:04:17,690
That's right.

101
00:04:17,690 --> 00:04:19,570
There's others that you could do, of course.

102
00:04:19,570 --> 00:04:20,390
Yes.

103
00:04:20,390 --> 00:04:21,709
OK.

104
00:04:21,709 --> 00:04:25,390
So the other thing I will talk about before I start--

105
00:04:25,390 --> 00:04:27,810
most of our discussion today will be hardware agnostic.

106
00:04:27,810 --> 00:04:30,650
For the most part, once you understand the basic concepts,

107
00:04:30,650 --> 00:04:33,670
you'll be able to apply it to any hardware substrate.

108
00:04:33,670 --> 00:04:36,250
But I do want to briefly, before we start,

109
00:04:36,250 --> 00:04:38,870
just talk about the low level hardware stack.

110
00:04:38,870 --> 00:04:41,110
Both because it's interesting.

111
00:04:41,110 --> 00:04:43,030
But also because I think it leads

112
00:04:43,030 --> 00:04:45,750
to different parallelization strategies

113
00:04:45,750 --> 00:04:48,910
across different model training companies.

114
00:04:48,910 --> 00:04:50,310
So remember the TPU.

115
00:04:50,310 --> 00:04:52,470
Right, TPU is Google's accelerator.

116
00:04:52,470 --> 00:04:54,690
And I mentioned during the GPU lecture,

117
00:04:54,690 --> 00:04:57,185
a TPU is like a lightweight GPU.

118
00:04:57,185 --> 00:04:58,810
But what's different is the networking.

119
00:04:58,810 --> 00:05:00,268
So how is the networking different?

120
00:05:00,268 --> 00:05:05,110
Well, in one slide, the TPU network

121
00:05:05,110 --> 00:05:06,870
is what's called a toroidal mesh.

122
00:05:06,870 --> 00:05:08,590
Right, so you can think about a grid

123
00:05:08,590 --> 00:05:10,598
and then allowing the ends of the grid

124
00:05:10,598 --> 00:05:11,890
to communicate with each other.

125
00:05:11,890 --> 00:05:13,348
It's a little bit more complicated.

126
00:05:13,348 --> 00:05:15,390
It's a 3D picture.

127
00:05:15,390 --> 00:05:18,150
Which, Marcel, you have the visualization, right?

128
00:05:18,150 --> 00:05:21,370
Can you post that in the lecture channel?

129
00:05:21,370 --> 00:05:21,870
Yeah.

130
00:05:21,870 --> 00:05:24,037
So there's a fun web visualization that you can look

131
00:05:24,037 --> 00:05:27,710
at that visualizes the toroidal mesh in 3D, which is very cool.

132
00:05:27,710 --> 00:05:30,650
But your mental model, I think, can roughly be this picture.

133
00:05:30,650 --> 00:05:32,450
You can forget the more complex one.

134
00:05:32,450 --> 00:05:35,387
All the chips are networked to neighbors and the neighbors wrap

135
00:05:35,387 --> 00:05:35,970
around, right.

136
00:05:35,970 --> 00:05:37,610
So what does this let you do?

137
00:05:37,610 --> 00:05:39,430
This lets you have a very simple networking

138
00:05:39,430 --> 00:05:41,787
topology that can be indefinitely scaled up very

139
00:05:41,787 --> 00:05:42,370
simply, right.

140
00:05:42,370 --> 00:05:45,790
The number of neighbors you have is the same, no matter

141
00:05:45,790 --> 00:05:48,670
how large your network gets.

142
00:05:48,670 --> 00:05:51,970
In contrast, the GPU networking philosophy is very different.

143
00:05:51,970 --> 00:05:54,490
It's really much more of an all to all philosophy.

144
00:05:54,490 --> 00:05:57,350
And the way that it's networked is it's like a tree.

145
00:05:57,350 --> 00:05:58,610
It's often called a fat tree.

146
00:05:58,610 --> 00:06:01,090
You have your GPUs connected very,

147
00:06:01,090 --> 00:06:02,770
very quickly at the lowest layers.

148
00:06:02,770 --> 00:06:05,850
And then you have groups of GPUs that are connected in a pod.

149
00:06:05,850 --> 00:06:07,950
And then the pods might have spine switches

150
00:06:07,950 --> 00:06:09,990
that allow them to communicate to each other.

151
00:06:09,990 --> 00:06:13,910
And this means that as the number of nodes grows,

152
00:06:13,910 --> 00:06:15,410
the tree gets bigger and bigger.

153
00:06:15,410 --> 00:06:18,330
Your communication costs is going to get higher,

154
00:06:18,330 --> 00:06:21,270
or your communication topology will get more complicated,

155
00:06:21,270 --> 00:06:25,470
because you're connecting all these all to all as you go.

156
00:06:25,470 --> 00:06:27,630
Now, this difference in philosophy

157
00:06:27,630 --> 00:06:30,290
means that if you're communicating only to neighbors,

158
00:06:30,290 --> 00:06:31,170
TPUs are great.

159
00:06:31,170 --> 00:06:32,910
They're cost effective.

160
00:06:32,910 --> 00:06:35,190
And you can make each connection more beefy,

161
00:06:35,190 --> 00:06:36,930
right, with the same power consumption.

162
00:06:36,930 --> 00:06:40,250
But if you're connecting in very random ways, very stochastic,

163
00:06:40,250 --> 00:06:42,462
unpredictable ways, then this GPU networking

164
00:06:42,462 --> 00:06:43,670
will be more flexible, right.

165
00:06:43,670 --> 00:06:46,950
So there's this very different design philosophy

166
00:06:46,950 --> 00:06:49,010
that you see between TPUs and GPUs.

167
00:06:49,010 --> 00:06:51,990
And I'll try to mention where this shows up

168
00:06:51,990 --> 00:06:55,590
as we go through the parallelism lecture.

169
00:06:55,590 --> 00:06:58,957
And I posted this into the lecture chat last lecture.

170
00:06:58,957 --> 00:07:00,790
Because someone asked, what's the difference

171
00:07:00,790 --> 00:07:02,310
between these two and so on?

172
00:07:02,310 --> 00:07:05,710
Right, but just to back up my point, Bill Daley and Jeff Dean

173
00:07:05,710 --> 00:07:08,350
were talking about the different hardware and what

174
00:07:08,350 --> 00:07:10,630
are they good for, right.

175
00:07:10,630 --> 00:07:12,950
GPUs are good for things like mixture of experts, where

176
00:07:12,950 --> 00:07:15,550
the communication is much less clear because tokens

177
00:07:15,550 --> 00:07:17,710
route to different experts.

178
00:07:17,710 --> 00:07:19,950
TPUs are great if you have this dense model

179
00:07:19,950 --> 00:07:23,150
that you're doing very predictable partitions of.

180
00:07:23,150 --> 00:07:27,390
I'll talk about that in an idea called tensor parallelism later

181
00:07:27,390 --> 00:07:28,370
in this lecture.

182
00:07:28,370 --> 00:07:31,970
And so these hardware things are good for different workloads.

183
00:07:31,970 --> 00:07:35,110
And this was where I was going to leave it for the lecture

184
00:07:35,110 --> 00:07:37,750
until this morning, when Google decided

185
00:07:37,750 --> 00:07:42,190
that they were going to announce TPU8i and t this morning.

186
00:07:42,190 --> 00:07:44,910
And then I looked at this as I was preparing my lecture,

187
00:07:44,910 --> 00:07:46,750
and I said, oh my god, TPU8i?

188
00:07:46,750 --> 00:07:48,650
That's a tree topology.

189
00:07:48,650 --> 00:07:51,710
They've switched to a much more all to all connection.

190
00:07:51,710 --> 00:07:54,630
And if you think about it, it makes sense.

191
00:07:54,630 --> 00:07:57,270
Modern day language models are MoEs.

192
00:07:57,270 --> 00:07:58,990
And if you're going to serve a MoE,

193
00:07:58,990 --> 00:08:00,790
you're going to have all sorts of bandwidth

194
00:08:00,790 --> 00:08:04,210
just flying around as you route tokens to different experts.

195
00:08:04,210 --> 00:08:07,750
You probably do want much more of an all to all

196
00:08:07,750 --> 00:08:09,940
connectivity where, at inference time,

197
00:08:09,940 --> 00:08:11,690
these communications are real bottlenecks.

198
00:08:11,690 --> 00:08:14,390
You want to deal with them.

199
00:08:14,390 --> 00:08:18,230
And even at training time, the TPU8t, which is a training chip,

200
00:08:18,230 --> 00:08:22,690
the cross rack connectivity looks a lot more like a GPU.

201
00:08:22,690 --> 00:08:25,230
There's much more like an all-to-all style

202
00:08:25,230 --> 00:08:28,050
switch that's being built out called the Virgo network.

203
00:08:28,050 --> 00:08:31,310
That's a higher level networking stack.

204
00:08:31,310 --> 00:08:34,049
So I think there's really interesting things happening.

205
00:08:34,049 --> 00:08:36,350
It's a little bit of a convergent evolution

206
00:08:36,350 --> 00:08:40,110
across both TPUs and GPUs, where really the workloads are

207
00:08:40,110 --> 00:08:43,169
defining the network that we need to have.

208
00:08:43,169 --> 00:08:43,669
OK.

209
00:08:43,669 --> 00:08:47,270
And then the final thing about hardware--

210
00:08:47,270 --> 00:08:51,390
I just love talking about what's happening in the Chinese AI

211
00:08:51,390 --> 00:08:55,670
scene-- is this idea of--I think in the GPU lecture, someone

212
00:08:55,670 --> 00:08:59,150
asked, if SRAM is so good, why don't you just all use SRAM?

213
00:08:59,150 --> 00:09:00,130
Only SRAM chip.

214
00:09:00,130 --> 00:09:01,130
And that's Grok, right?

215
00:09:01,130 --> 00:09:02,190
It works really well.

216
00:09:02,190 --> 00:09:04,470
Now, some of you might be thinking,

217
00:09:04,470 --> 00:09:07,270
if all-to-all connectivity is good, why don't you just

218
00:09:07,270 --> 00:09:10,110
connect everything and connect them with really fast fiber

219
00:09:10,110 --> 00:09:10,930
optic chips, right?

220
00:09:10,930 --> 00:09:15,270
And then you would get the Huawei Ascend 910, right?

221
00:09:15,270 --> 00:09:18,735
So if you look at the specs of the Huawei Ascend,

222
00:09:18,735 --> 00:09:20,110
it's actually really interesting,

223
00:09:20,110 --> 00:09:23,070
because each chip is a lot worse than a H200.

224
00:09:23,070 --> 00:09:25,430
It's a lot slower at matmuls.

225
00:09:25,430 --> 00:09:27,850
It's slower in many ways.

226
00:09:27,850 --> 00:09:32,270
But then, if you're willing to dedicate a giant rack of fiber

227
00:09:32,270 --> 00:09:35,450
optic switches to connect a much larger number of chips,

228
00:09:35,450 --> 00:09:39,750
like in their case, 384 in a big rack,

229
00:09:39,750 --> 00:09:41,950
then maybe you can deal with the fact

230
00:09:41,950 --> 00:09:45,950
that each chip is slower by just connecting a much, much larger

231
00:09:45,950 --> 00:09:49,060
number of these guys, right.

232
00:09:49,060 --> 00:09:50,620
And then so I think what this shows

233
00:09:50,620 --> 00:09:52,400
us is a really interesting tradeoff.

234
00:09:52,400 --> 00:09:54,320
Because once you look at this architecture,

235
00:09:54,320 --> 00:09:57,180
you realize the power consumption of this thing

236
00:09:57,180 --> 00:09:58,440
is insanely high.

237
00:09:58,440 --> 00:10:02,100
It's four times that of the equivalent NVIDIA system.

238
00:10:02,100 --> 00:10:03,820
And so it's something like, well,

239
00:10:03,820 --> 00:10:05,760
if you're willing to pay the power cost,

240
00:10:05,760 --> 00:10:09,200
you can solve a lot of communication problems

241
00:10:09,200 --> 00:10:11,660
brute force, and you can scale out much more aggressively

242
00:10:11,660 --> 00:10:12,877
to 300 chips.

243
00:10:12,877 --> 00:10:14,460
But you're paying a power cost, right.

244
00:10:14,460 --> 00:10:16,420
It's similar to that SRAM story of,

245
00:10:16,420 --> 00:10:18,420
if you want to design a hardware that

246
00:10:18,420 --> 00:10:21,880
is efficient to use both power and manufacturing wise,

247
00:10:21,880 --> 00:10:23,130
you end up in a certain place.

248
00:10:23,130 --> 00:10:24,547
If you want to brute force things,

249
00:10:24,547 --> 00:10:26,100
you end up in a very different place.

250
00:10:26,100 --> 00:10:29,580
Right, so this is, I think, just a very interesting place

251
00:10:29,580 --> 00:10:33,060
to learn from about these hardware design tradeoffs.

252
00:10:33,060 --> 00:10:34,580
OK, good.

253
00:10:34,580 --> 00:10:37,260
So now, just to put this all together,

254
00:10:37,260 --> 00:10:40,500
the first thing you should think about is the new unit of compute

255
00:10:40,500 --> 00:10:41,520
is not the GPU.

256
00:10:41,520 --> 00:10:43,220
It's the entire data center.

257
00:10:43,220 --> 00:10:45,280
And we want to have several things.

258
00:10:45,280 --> 00:10:48,080
We want to be able to control the amount of memory we use.

259
00:10:48,080 --> 00:10:50,380
We want to be able to control the amount of compute

260
00:10:50,380 --> 00:10:51,680
that we can bring to bear.

261
00:10:51,680 --> 00:10:53,940
And we want all of these to be lossless.

262
00:10:53,940 --> 00:10:57,020
Right, we want to use all of the resources that we have.

263
00:10:57,020 --> 00:11:00,380
And then we're going to talk about the algorithms that

264
00:11:00,380 --> 00:11:03,140
will enable this through all these communication

265
00:11:03,140 --> 00:11:05,860
primitives components.

266
00:11:05,860 --> 00:11:07,900
OK, good.

267
00:11:07,900 --> 00:11:10,180
So now the second part of this lecture

268
00:11:10,180 --> 00:11:12,280
is going to be the bulk of the content.

269
00:11:12,280 --> 00:11:14,860
And these are the algorithms that we will use

270
00:11:14,860 --> 00:11:18,500
to parallelize language models.

271
00:11:18,500 --> 00:11:23,220
And there's going to be a couple of different important ideas.

272
00:11:23,220 --> 00:11:25,420
Actually, we should probably maybe even be grouped.

273
00:11:25,420 --> 00:11:28,420
Data parallel is one set of ideas for essentially

274
00:11:28,420 --> 00:11:30,740
taking your data that you're going to train on

275
00:11:30,740 --> 00:11:32,640
and distributing them across GPUs.

276
00:11:32,640 --> 00:11:34,723
So the data is the ones that move around.

277
00:11:34,723 --> 00:11:36,140
And then model parallelism, you're

278
00:11:36,140 --> 00:11:38,015
going to chop up different parts of the model

279
00:11:38,015 --> 00:11:39,380
and split them around.

280
00:11:39,380 --> 00:11:41,300
Turns out that the boundary between these two

281
00:11:41,300 --> 00:11:43,300
is a little leaky, because one of the algorithms

282
00:11:43,300 --> 00:11:45,500
here will actually cut parameters up, too.

283
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:47,945
But we will see why I've put all of these algorithms

284
00:11:47,945 --> 00:11:49,320
into these different bins, right.

285
00:11:49,320 --> 00:11:53,300
And conceptually, we're trying to both scale

286
00:11:53,300 --> 00:11:55,060
out compute and memory, and that's

287
00:11:55,060 --> 00:11:59,540
why we will need a whole bunch of these different tricks.

288
00:11:59,540 --> 00:12:00,860
Cool.

289
00:12:00,860 --> 00:12:01,360
All right.

290
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:04,360
So data parallelism, I think, is the easiest to understand.

291
00:12:04,360 --> 00:12:05,980
It is conceptually very simple, and I

292
00:12:05,980 --> 00:12:09,180
think it's the standard way that you would try to parallelize

293
00:12:09,180 --> 00:12:11,900
an algorithm like LLM training.

294
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:13,640
So forget that we're doing Adam.

295
00:12:13,640 --> 00:12:16,860
We're going to do just naive SGD for the moment.

296
00:12:16,860 --> 00:12:20,000
Then we have a capital B sized batch, right.

297
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:23,480
So each update step, I'm going to take capital B elements,

298
00:12:23,480 --> 00:12:25,760
and I'm going to sum up the gradients.

299
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:27,280
And I'll take my update, right.

300
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:28,900
So the most naive form of parallelism

301
00:12:28,900 --> 00:12:31,860
that one can do for this is to take my batch,

302
00:12:31,860 --> 00:12:34,323
B. I can cut it up across M machines.

303
00:12:34,323 --> 00:12:35,740
If they're divisible, each machine

304
00:12:35,740 --> 00:12:39,292
will get a smaller B over M sized batch.

305
00:12:39,292 --> 00:12:42,000
And then what I'm going to do is each machine computes gradients,

306
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:46,340
and then I will synchronize all the gradients to get my sum.

307
00:12:46,340 --> 00:12:47,160
OK, great.

308
00:12:47,160 --> 00:12:49,620
So compute scaling wise, this is perfect.

309
00:12:49,620 --> 00:12:52,340
As long as there's enough examples per GPU, we're good.

310
00:12:52,340 --> 00:12:53,800
Communications overhead.

311
00:12:53,800 --> 00:12:55,660
How much do we have to talk to each other?

312
00:12:55,660 --> 00:12:59,150
Well, every batch, we have to basically communicate

313
00:12:59,150 --> 00:13:00,900
two times the number of parameters, right.

314
00:13:00,900 --> 00:13:05,220
Because what I have to do is I have to basically communicate

315
00:13:05,220 --> 00:13:07,780
my gradients back and forth.

316
00:13:07,780 --> 00:13:10,920
OK, and then for memory scaling, there's absolutely none.

317
00:13:10,920 --> 00:13:13,640
Every GPU has the same copies of the models.

318
00:13:13,640 --> 00:13:16,300
You need to have the same sized activations

319
00:13:16,300 --> 00:13:21,260
so you don't save any memory at all in this scheme.

320
00:13:21,260 --> 00:13:23,280
So now what is the problem?

321
00:13:23,280 --> 00:13:26,000
Clearly, we've addressed compute to some extent.

322
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:27,680
But we have not addressed memory at all.

323
00:13:27,680 --> 00:13:30,700
So let's take a look at this memory problem

324
00:13:30,700 --> 00:13:33,780
and just do a little bit of accounting.

325
00:13:33,780 --> 00:13:36,938
Initially, it might seem that the memory situation is bad.

326
00:13:36,938 --> 00:13:38,980
But it turns out our memory situation is actually

327
00:13:38,980 --> 00:13:40,500
just terrible.

328
00:13:40,500 --> 00:13:43,420
And the reason why the memory situation is terrible

329
00:13:43,420 --> 00:13:46,460
is if you just do some parameter accounting, right,

330
00:13:46,460 --> 00:13:49,308
it just turns out you need to store a lot of stuff.

331
00:13:49,308 --> 00:13:51,600
Right, and not only do we need to store a lot of stuff,

332
00:13:51,600 --> 00:13:55,020
it's actually more than the parameters that we need to store.

333
00:13:55,020 --> 00:13:58,940
So roughly the rule of thumb-- it depends on your precision.

334
00:13:58,940 --> 00:14:00,680
But the rule of thumb, you might say,

335
00:14:00,680 --> 00:14:03,740
is that we need something like five copies of weights

336
00:14:03,740 --> 00:14:06,980
and 16 bytes per parameter to store our model.

337
00:14:06,980 --> 00:14:10,580
So we will need to, of course, store our model parameters.

338
00:14:10,580 --> 00:14:13,098
We need a place to put our gradients, right.

339
00:14:13,098 --> 00:14:15,640
The accumulator before we put them into the model parameters.

340
00:14:15,640 --> 00:14:18,340
So we need to store those guys.

341
00:14:18,340 --> 00:14:21,420
But actually, you need to maybe store more stuff.

342
00:14:21,420 --> 00:14:24,860
You might need to have a higher precision accumulator that you

343
00:14:24,860 --> 00:14:26,740
accumulate into in SGD.

344
00:14:26,740 --> 00:14:29,347
This might be temporary, but you might need this.

345
00:14:29,347 --> 00:14:31,180
And if you're doing Adam, and this is really

346
00:14:31,180 --> 00:14:33,500
where the bad stuff comes in, you're

347
00:14:33,500 --> 00:14:35,860
going to need to track both the first and second moments

348
00:14:35,860 --> 00:14:36,760
of your gradients.

349
00:14:36,760 --> 00:14:38,593
And you need to track them over time, right.

350
00:14:38,593 --> 00:14:40,700
So you need two of these.

351
00:14:40,700 --> 00:14:42,780
And unfortunately, you might need

352
00:14:42,780 --> 00:14:45,300
to store them in high precision depending on your stability

353
00:14:45,300 --> 00:14:46,160
characteristics.

354
00:14:46,160 --> 00:14:48,820
And these might be quite expensive, right.

355
00:14:48,820 --> 00:14:52,382
And we generally call these things optimizer state.

356
00:14:52,382 --> 00:14:54,340
Right, the thing that you're accumulating into.

357
00:14:54,340 --> 00:14:55,840
The first moment, the second moment.

358
00:14:55,840 --> 00:14:57,240
Right, and these are actually quite expensive.

359
00:14:57,240 --> 00:15:04,620
If you look at the accounting, this is most of the cost memory wise of doing an SGD update.

360
00:15:04,620 --> 00:15:09,820
And so I think one of the really elegant and nice things

361
00:15:09,820 --> 00:15:12,780
that you can do in this space is to start

362
00:15:12,780 --> 00:15:15,540
cutting up the amount of memory you need

363
00:15:15,540 --> 00:15:18,000
and distributing them across the accelerators,

364
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,700
but doing so in this structured way that gets you

365
00:15:20,700 --> 00:15:23,140
these gains for basically free.

366
00:15:23,140 --> 00:15:26,340
So as I said in the last slide, the optimizer state

367
00:15:26,340 --> 00:15:27,520
is most of your memory.

368
00:15:27,520 --> 00:15:29,880
So here, the optimizer state is green.

369
00:15:29,880 --> 00:15:31,580
That's most of your memory.

370
00:15:31,580 --> 00:15:34,963
Orange is your gradients, and blue is your parameters.

371
00:15:34,963 --> 00:15:37,380
And notice how parameters and gradients are the same size,

372
00:15:37,380 --> 00:15:39,650
and your state is bigger, right.

373
00:15:39,650 --> 00:15:41,370
If you do naive data parallel, you're

374
00:15:41,370 --> 00:15:44,830
just going to replicate all of this state across the GPUs.

375
00:15:44,830 --> 00:15:46,450
And this is not so good, right.

376
00:15:46,450 --> 00:15:49,050
Because you've, in some sense, consumed way

377
00:15:49,050 --> 00:15:51,330
more memory than you need and your memory consumption

378
00:15:51,330 --> 00:15:53,670
is linear with your number of accelerators.

379
00:15:53,670 --> 00:15:55,690
Not so good, right.

380
00:15:55,690 --> 00:15:58,010
What we can do instead is perhaps

381
00:15:58,010 --> 00:16:02,630
we can shard the optimizer state onto different GPUs.

382
00:16:02,630 --> 00:16:04,370
And this would be a big savings.

383
00:16:04,370 --> 00:16:08,070
If we shard different other parts of what we need to track,

384
00:16:08,070 --> 00:16:09,950
that would be also big savings.

385
00:16:09,950 --> 00:16:13,210
Maybe we can also shard the gradients into different GPUs.

386
00:16:13,210 --> 00:16:15,730
Or in the extreme, maybe we can shard everything

387
00:16:15,730 --> 00:16:17,430
into different accelerators.

388
00:16:17,430 --> 00:16:19,770
And then you can see that the implied memory

389
00:16:19,770 --> 00:16:22,890
gains on the very right would be quite dramatic as we go all

390
00:16:22,890 --> 00:16:25,770
the way down from 120 to 1.9.

391
00:16:25,770 --> 00:16:29,090
Now, the main thing that we will study now

392
00:16:29,090 --> 00:16:31,170
is, what is the cost of doing this?

393
00:16:31,170 --> 00:16:33,770
Right, you might imagine, there's no free lunch.

394
00:16:33,770 --> 00:16:35,668
Baseline is going to be the fastest.

395
00:16:35,668 --> 00:16:37,210
And as we go downwards to here, we're

396
00:16:37,210 --> 00:16:39,770
going to have to pay a big communication cost in order

397
00:16:39,770 --> 00:16:41,387
to achieve these memory savings.

398
00:16:41,387 --> 00:16:43,970
I think the remarkable thing is going to be that a lot of this

399
00:16:43,970 --> 00:16:46,450
will be free.

400
00:16:46,450 --> 00:16:48,610
But I'm going to walk through this step by step.

401
00:16:48,610 --> 00:16:51,915
So let's start with what people call ZeRO stage 1.

402
00:16:51,915 --> 00:16:54,290
So there's going to be three stages corresponding to each

403
00:16:54,290 --> 00:16:55,310
of these steps.

404
00:16:55,310 --> 00:16:57,270
And so stage 1 is going to be the simplest.

405
00:16:57,270 --> 00:16:59,730
The only thing I will shard is my optimizer state.

406
00:16:59,730 --> 00:17:03,050
And I will shard it across the GPUs, OK.

407
00:17:03,050 --> 00:17:04,970
Everyone is going to have both the parameters

408
00:17:04,970 --> 00:17:06,690
and the gradients.

409
00:17:06,690 --> 00:17:09,130
And so what you can think of is each worker

410
00:17:09,130 --> 00:17:11,210
is going to be responsible for updating

411
00:17:11,210 --> 00:17:12,829
one slice of the parameter.

412
00:17:12,829 --> 00:17:15,690
So if I'm GPU number 0, my responsibility

413
00:17:15,690 --> 00:17:18,490
is updating this very first slice over here.

414
00:17:18,490 --> 00:17:23,450
And I'm not responsible for any of the other updates.

415
00:17:23,450 --> 00:17:24,690
So how would it work?

416
00:17:24,690 --> 00:17:27,470
Right, so what we're going to do is everyone computes.

417
00:17:27,470 --> 00:17:29,390
Everyone has a different data item, right.

418
00:17:29,390 --> 00:17:32,130
So they're going to compute a full gradient on their data

419
00:17:32,130 --> 00:17:32,630
item.

420
00:17:32,630 --> 00:17:33,770
That's the first step.

421
00:17:33,770 --> 00:17:36,230
Now I'm going to take the gradient I computed

422
00:17:36,230 --> 00:17:38,778
and I'm going to reduce scatter the gradients onto all

423
00:17:38,778 --> 00:17:39,570
the other machines.

424
00:17:39,570 --> 00:17:41,570
Because everyone else needs my gradients, right?

425
00:17:41,570 --> 00:17:43,510
But they don't need all the gradients.

426
00:17:43,510 --> 00:17:46,750
They only need the gradients associated with their update.

427
00:17:46,750 --> 00:17:50,830
Right, so every worker is going to get from me, my gradient,

428
00:17:50,830 --> 00:17:53,190
but they're part of the parameter space, right.

429
00:17:53,190 --> 00:17:55,450
So this is going to be equivalent to a number

430
00:17:55,450 --> 00:17:57,040
of parameters communication costs.

431
00:17:57,040 --> 00:17:59,290
Because I'm sending my gradient, but I'm cutting it up

432
00:17:59,290 --> 00:18:02,050
and sending it into each GPU.

433
00:18:02,050 --> 00:18:04,870
Now each machine is going to be able to now do their update.

434
00:18:04,870 --> 00:18:07,130
They have their parameters, they have their gradients

435
00:18:07,130 --> 00:18:08,550
that they got from everybody else,

436
00:18:08,550 --> 00:18:10,967
and they have their own state that they've tracked, right.

437
00:18:10,967 --> 00:18:13,630
And now, the parameters have been updated.

438
00:18:13,630 --> 00:18:15,510
And now I'm going to all gather them back.

439
00:18:15,510 --> 00:18:17,050
Right, I was responsible for, let's

440
00:18:17,050 --> 00:18:19,050
say, one fourth of the parameters.

441
00:18:19,050 --> 00:18:21,368
所以那些更新后的参数，我会发送回去

442
00:18:21,368 --> 00:18:22,410
给其他所有人，对吧。

443
00:18:22,410 --> 00:18:25,930
所以这是一个全收集，对吧。

444
00:18:25,930 --> 00:18:28,610
所以这就是等价性有用的地方。

445
00:18:28,610 --> 00:18:30,930
我做了多少次操作？

446
00:18:30,930 --> 00:18:35,210
在朴素的数据并行情况下，我需要做的是

447
00:18:35,210 --> 00:18:36,790
我必须做一次全归约。

448
00:18:36,790 --> 00:18:39,658
对吧，我必须和所有人交换所有梯度

449
00:18:39,658 --> 00:18:40,950
然后进行更新，对吧。

450
00:18:40,950 --> 00:18:43,890
所以通信成本是参数量的 2 倍。

451
00:18:43,890 --> 00:18:47,190
现在在 ZeRO 第一阶段，我做了两个操作。

452
00:18:47,190 --> 00:18:49,790
一个是归约散射，把梯度发送出去

453
00:18:49,790 --> 00:18:52,230
然后是一个全收集，收集更新后的参数。

454
00:18:52,230 --> 00:18:54,770
但我们已经说过，归约散射和全收集

455
00:18:54,770 --> 00:18:56,610
等价于一次全归约。

456
00:18:56,610 --> 00:19:00,850
所以 ZeRO 第一阶段将具有完全相同的通信

457
00:19:00,850 --> 00:19:03,710
特征，与朴素 DDP 相同。

458
00:19:03,710 --> 00:19:04,710
对吧，所以这是免费的。

459
00:19:04,710 --> 00:19:05,630
免费的内存节省。

460
00:19:05,630 --> 00:19:06,370
太好了，对吧？

461
00:19:06,370 --> 00:19:07,768
然后我们看看内存。

462
00:19:07,768 --> 00:19:09,310
我们基本上把参数

463
00:19:09,310 --> 00:19:12,570
以及任何优化器状态，都除以了 n 个 GPU，

464
00:19:12,570 --> 00:19:13,070
对吧。

465
00:19:13,070 --> 00:19:15,330
太好了，太好了，太好了。

466
00:19:15,330 --> 00:19:16,850
现在，我们能做更多吗？

467
00:19:16,850 --> 00:19:19,290
ZeRO 第二阶段现在会说，

468
00:19:19,290 --> 00:19:21,330
我要把梯度拆分开。

469
00:19:21,330 --> 00:19:25,810
然后我还要对它进行分片。

470
00:19:25,810 --> 00:19:27,463
现在，这会更棘手

471
00:19:27,463 --> 00:19:29,130
因为之前我依赖于这样一个事实

472
00:19:29,130 --> 00:19:31,390
我可以计算整个梯度，

473
00:19:31,390 --> 00:19:33,590
现在我却甚至无法物化整个梯度。

474
00:19:33,590 --> 00:19:35,650
那我该怎么办。

475
00:19:35,650 --> 00:19:38,810
好吧，这其实只是一个系统技巧。

476
00:19:38,810 --> 00:19:40,920
当我反向遍历我的网络时，

477
00:19:40,920 --> 00:19:42,670
我不需要计算完整的梯度。

478
00:19:42,670 --> 00:19:47,410
我可以在进行过程中把梯度发送给相关的工作节点。

479
00:19:47,410 --> 00:19:51,730
所以这里的关键步骤是，现在不再物化

480
00:19:51,730 --> 00:19:53,690
梯度向量一次性全部，我

481
00:19:53,690 --> 00:19:56,010
going to walk backwards through the compute graph.

482
00:19:56,010 --> 00:19:57,850
And after computing a layer's gradients,

483
00:19:57,850 --> 00:19:59,795
I'm going to immediately just reduce

484
00:19:59,795 --> 00:20:01,670
that to send that to the right worker, right.

485
00:20:01,670 --> 00:20:04,548
And once gradients aren't needed in the backwards graph,

486
00:20:04,548 --> 00:20:05,590
just immediately free it.

487
00:20:05,590 --> 00:20:07,650
Right, so you're now incrementally computing

488
00:20:07,650 --> 00:20:09,370
and sending gradients as you go.

489
00:20:09,370 --> 00:20:12,610
And as you can see, doing it incrementally or doing it

490
00:20:12,610 --> 00:20:14,230
all at once, same thing, right?

491
00:20:14,230 --> 00:20:16,310
So it doesn't really make a difference.

492
00:20:16,310 --> 00:20:19,770
So now you can, at the end, once again all-gather the parameters.

493
00:20:19,770 --> 00:20:21,730
I've started the gradients in some sense

494
00:20:21,730 --> 00:20:25,610
without incurring any additional costs, right.

495
00:20:25,610 --> 00:20:28,850
OK, so the last and the most hairy thing-- and this always,

496
00:20:28,850 --> 00:20:31,970
when I first learned about this, seemed like total magic--

497
00:20:31,970 --> 00:20:35,070
is we've gotten something for free so far,

498
00:20:35,070 --> 00:20:36,390
so let's keep pushing it.

499
00:20:36,390 --> 00:20:39,250
I'm going to shard the parameters as well all the way

500
00:20:39,250 --> 00:20:41,250
across all the states.

501
00:20:41,250 --> 00:20:43,370
And so we're going to use the same kind of idea

502
00:20:43,370 --> 00:20:45,207
that we used in ZeRO stage 2.

503
00:20:45,207 --> 00:20:47,290
And the idea, remember, was I'm going to do things

504
00:20:47,290 --> 00:20:49,273
incrementally, right.

505
00:20:49,273 --> 00:20:50,690
And so what I'm going to do is I'm

506
00:20:50,690 --> 00:20:54,410
going to send and receive parameters on demand while

507
00:20:54,410 --> 00:20:55,870
stepping through the compute graph.

508
00:20:55,870 --> 00:20:59,210
And this is really tricky, because each GPU only

509
00:20:59,210 --> 00:21:01,410
sees a slice of both the parameters, gradients,

510
00:21:01,410 --> 00:21:04,970
and optimizer states at any one time, right.

511
00:21:04,970 --> 00:21:07,550
And this is ZeRO stage 3, also known as FSDP.

512
00:21:07,550 --> 00:21:10,750
If you've ever parallelized anything in a Torch library,

513
00:21:10,750 --> 00:21:13,970
you've probably dealt with this before.

514
00:21:13,970 --> 00:21:17,050
So the way that it works under the hood

515
00:21:17,050 --> 00:21:18,570
is what you're going to do is you're

516
00:21:18,570 --> 00:21:22,310
going to do two all-gathers and one reduce-scatter as you go.

517
00:21:22,310 --> 00:21:25,230
So we're going to step from left to right here.

518
00:21:25,230 --> 00:21:28,790
And this is going to be the entire update process.

519
00:21:28,790 --> 00:21:32,030
So what you do is you're going to load your model.

520
00:21:32,030 --> 00:21:34,850
You have your two GPUs at the top and the bottom.

521
00:21:34,850 --> 00:21:38,390
You're first going to gather your weights for this one layer.

522
00:21:38,390 --> 00:21:40,155
I'm going to forward this one layer.

523
00:21:40,155 --> 00:21:42,530
And then I can free my weights, because I've already done

524
00:21:42,530 --> 00:21:43,322
the forward, right.

525
00:21:43,322 --> 00:21:45,130
I don't need these parameters anymore.

526
00:21:45,130 --> 00:21:47,490
Now, let's say I only have one layer.

527
00:21:47,490 --> 00:21:49,910
Now I can move to the backwards process.

528
00:21:49,910 --> 00:21:52,170
Now to do the backwards step, I need the activations,

529
00:21:52,170 --> 00:21:53,250
which I've kept.

530
00:21:53,250 --> 00:21:55,330
And I need the parameters for this layer, which

531
00:21:55,330 --> 00:21:57,570
I can all-gather on demand.

532
00:21:57,570 --> 00:21:59,590
I all-gather it, I do the backwards.

533
00:21:59,590 --> 00:22:02,100
And then I scatter the gradients back right when I get them.

534
00:22:02,100 --> 00:22:03,350
Right, so I got the gradients.

535
00:22:03,350 --> 00:22:04,863
I reduce-scatter the gradients out.

536
00:22:04,863 --> 00:22:06,030
And then I free the weights.

537
00:22:06,030 --> 00:22:08,530
I don't need them anymore, and I repeat this process, right.

538
00:22:08,530 --> 00:22:10,750
So I grab the parameters as I need them.

539
00:22:10,750 --> 00:22:12,230
I compute my backwards.

540
00:22:12,230 --> 00:22:14,930
I send the gradients out, and I repeat this process.

541
00:22:14,930 --> 00:22:16,630
And then I update my weights.

542
00:22:16,630 --> 00:22:18,650
All done, right.

543
00:22:18,650 --> 00:22:20,287
So what's the communication cost?

544
00:22:20,287 --> 00:22:21,870
Right, how many all-gathers did we do?

545
00:22:21,870 --> 00:22:25,770
We did two all-gathers, one reduce-scatter.

546
00:22:25,770 --> 00:22:30,100
Also, so we have one extra all-gather, which seems bad.

547
00:22:30,100 --> 00:22:30,860
That's one thing.

548
00:22:30,860 --> 00:22:33,240
The second thing is this seems a little crazy in terms

549
00:22:33,240 --> 00:22:35,560
of the overhead, because we're doing communication

550
00:22:35,560 --> 00:22:36,360
all the time.

551
00:22:36,360 --> 00:22:39,600
Every layer, we're doing this communication.

552
00:22:39,600 --> 00:22:43,960
You might think that this is extremely expensive and bad.

553
00:22:43,960 --> 00:22:45,760
But I think one of the things that's

554
00:22:45,760 --> 00:22:49,240
counterintuitive and important is

555
00:22:49,240 --> 00:22:53,520
ZeRO stage 3 uses two really important ideas to make

556
00:22:53,520 --> 00:22:56,200
this essentially overhead free.

557
00:22:56,200 --> 00:22:58,160
So the first one, which we've already seen,

558
00:22:58,160 --> 00:23:01,030
is this idea that we're going to sweep through the compute graph,

559
00:23:01,030 --> 00:23:03,280
and then we're going to do the necessary communication

560
00:23:03,280 --> 00:23:05,120
and then immediately free the memory.

561
00:23:05,120 --> 00:23:07,800
对，这已经是我们体验过的一个思路。

562
00:23:07,800 --> 00:23:10,720
另一个思路，我还没有讲过，

563
00:23:10,720 --> 00:23:14,040
就是我们要把通信和计算重叠起来

564
00:23:14,040 --> 00:23:16,180
这个过程的成本，对吧。

565
00:23:16,180 --> 00:23:19,460
这个一开始很难解释。

566
00:23:19,460 --> 00:23:22,600
所以我觉得这张图是从这篇论文里拿的，

567
00:23:22,600 --> 00:23:27,440
我觉得它是在解释 PyTorch FSDP 架构。

568
00:23:27,440 --> 00:23:32,478
它的工作方式是——我们有不同的

569
00:23:32,478 --> 00:23:33,020
流。

570
00:23:33,020 --> 00:23:34,620
这是 CPU 里正在发生的事情。

571
00:23:34,620 --> 00:23:36,910
这是 GPU 里正在进行的计算，

572
00:23:36,910 --> 00:23:39,160
然后这是 GPU 里正在进行的通信，

573
00:23:39,160 --> 00:23:41,000
在最底下这里。

574
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:44,880
FSDP 的工作方式是，我必须先拿到我的参数

575
00:23:44,880 --> 00:23:46,460
才能进行计算。

576
00:23:46,460 --> 00:23:48,660
所以我要做全收集 0，第 0 层，对吧。

577
00:23:48,660 --> 00:23:51,560
一旦我全收集完了，我现在就可以做前向。

578
00:23:51,560 --> 00:23:53,280
但在我做前向的时候，注意

579
00:23:53,280 --> 00:23:56,140
我在请求下一层的参数。

580
00:23:56,140 --> 00:23:59,280
我在做这个计算的同时做全收集 1。

581
00:23:59,280 --> 00:24:02,680
现在我全收集了 1，我就可以做前向 1，对吧。

582
00:24:02,680 --> 00:24:06,420
而且我可以在我开始这个流程的同时开始全收集 2。

583
00:24:06,420 --> 00:24:10,240
所以我把计算和通信重叠起来了

584
00:24:10,240 --> 00:24:11,620
在我做这件事的时候。

585
00:24:11,620 --> 00:24:14,420
我可能花一些时间释放我的参数。

586
00:24:14,420 --> 00:24:17,980
我也可能花一些时间计算另一个前向。

587
00:24:17,980 --> 00:24:20,580
例如，如果你复用那个 W0，

588
00:24:20,580 --> 00:24:24,240
我复用了这个前向 0 两次。

589
00:24:24,240 --> 00:24:25,720
所以这让你能够做到的是

590
00:24:25,720 --> 00:24:28,320
如果你有足够的计算，而且你的网络

591
00:24:28,320 --> 00:24:31,640
足够快，通过重叠通信和计算，

592
00:24:31,640 --> 00:24:32,320
对吧。

593
00:24:32,320 --> 00:24:34,368
FSDP 基本上可以是免费的。

594
00:24:34,368 --> 00:24:35,660
对，你确实会看到一些气泡。

595
00:24:35,660 --> 00:24:36,920
这些是开销。

596
00:24:36,920 --> 00:24:39,880
但如果你的通信非常快，而你的计算

597
00:24:39,880 --> 00:24:41,920
非常大，对吧，你可以很容易

598
00:24:41,920 --> 00:24:44,942
看出计算如何能比通信跑得更快——

599
00:24:44,942 --> 00:24:46,400
抱歉，是计算可以花

600
00:24:46,400 --> 00:24:48,480
比通信更长的时间，而你

601
00:24:48,480 --> 00:24:52,320
end up having this really great improvement in memory usage

602
00:24:52,320 --> 00:24:57,040
without paying for almost any amount of memory use.

603
00:24:57,040 --> 00:25:01,840
OK, so when we look at the different ZeRO stages,

604
00:25:01,840 --> 00:25:04,800
right, DDP, the naive data parallel,

605
00:25:04,800 --> 00:25:06,800
costs 2 times parameters.

606
00:25:06,800 --> 00:25:09,280
Right, it was a single all-reduce.

607
00:25:09,280 --> 00:25:11,260
The two ZeRO stages, 1 and 2, right,

608
00:25:11,260 --> 00:25:13,780
the ones that shard gradient and optimizer state,

609
00:25:13,780 --> 00:25:15,840
this is free in the literal sense,

610
00:25:15,840 --> 00:25:18,920
because it has the same amount of communication cost, right.

611
00:25:18,920 --> 00:25:21,360
It's just the identity of all-reduce

612
00:25:21,360 --> 00:25:25,560
being equivalent to two operations, right.

613
00:25:25,560 --> 00:25:28,000
Now, ZeRO stage 3 is technically a little bit more

614
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:28,740
communications.

615
00:25:28,740 --> 00:25:30,680
You need an extra all-gather, right.

616
00:25:30,680 --> 00:25:33,800
But it's not so bad because of the really clever ways

617
00:25:33,800 --> 00:25:36,880
in which all these libraries can hide the communication cost

618
00:25:36,880 --> 00:25:38,060
underneath the computation.

619
00:25:38,060 --> 00:25:40,700
So in practice, FSDP, if you run it,

620
00:25:40,700 --> 00:25:42,840
you're going to see GPU utilization that's

621
00:25:42,840 --> 00:25:46,300
very close to just the single GPU performance.

622
00:25:46,300 --> 00:25:50,195
It is actually quite remarkable how good FSDP is.

623
00:25:50,195 --> 00:25:51,820
And it's also very conceptually simple.

624
00:25:51,820 --> 00:25:54,960
You will actually have to write an FSDP implementation

625
00:25:54,960 --> 00:25:57,000
as part of your assignment.

626
00:25:57,000 --> 00:26:00,560
And you can just write a wrapper that will wrap any module

627
00:26:00,560 --> 00:26:02,620
and turn it into its FSDP version.

628
00:26:02,620 --> 00:26:04,600
Right, conceptually, all you're going to do

629
00:26:04,600 --> 00:26:07,680
is do a bunch of all-gathers, compute, free, and then repeat

630
00:26:07,680 --> 00:26:10,320
on the backwards pass.

631
00:26:10,320 --> 00:26:12,840
OK, good.

632
00:26:12,840 --> 00:26:14,160
Yes?

633
00:26:16,520 --> 00:26:19,200
gradients from the next GPU to calculate the gradients

634
00:26:19,200 --> 00:26:21,320
for the previous GPU, right?

635
00:26:21,320 --> 00:26:25,080
When you say-- so we're not--

636
00:26:25,080 --> 00:26:26,760
I think what you just described, which

637
00:26:26,760 --> 00:26:29,480
is taking gradients from one GPU to the next, that's

638
00:26:29,480 --> 00:26:30,580
closer to pipelining.

639
00:26:30,580 --> 00:26:32,720
Pipelining would be if one layer was on one GPU

640
00:26:32,720 --> 00:26:34,680
and another layer was on another GPU.

641
00:26:34,680 --> 00:26:35,580
That's not the case.

642
00:26:35,580 --> 00:26:42,200
So in all these cases, every GPU goes through the entire model,

643
00:26:42,200 --> 00:26:43,920
right, from the start to finish.

644
00:26:43,920 --> 00:26:46,440
But the difference is, no GPU is going

645
00:26:46,440 --> 00:26:49,000
to hold the entire parameters at the same time.

646
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:51,280
And so what we're going to do is if I only

647
00:26:51,280 --> 00:26:54,900
have part of the network that doesn't have layer 0,

648
00:26:54,900 --> 00:26:57,680
I'm going to demand the layer 0 parameters first

649
00:26:57,680 --> 00:26:59,060
and then do the full computation.

650
00:26:59,060 --> 00:27:01,018
And then I'll move on to the next layer, right.

651
00:27:01,018 --> 00:27:02,960
So it's the same computation.

652
00:27:02,960 --> 00:27:05,860
It's the same single GPU computation as naive data parallel.

653
00:27:05,860 --> 00:27:08,460
But you can just think of it as request parameters,

654
00:27:08,460 --> 00:27:12,020
free parameters in between every computational operation.

655
00:27:12,020 --> 00:27:12,520
Yes?

656
00:27:12,520 --> 00:27:15,758
So are you saying that every GPU has a part of every layer,

657
00:27:15,758 --> 00:27:17,300
and that's what the all-gather's for?

658
00:27:17,300 --> 00:27:17,900
Yes, that's right.

659
00:27:17,900 --> 00:27:19,150
So the picture is here, right.

660
00:27:19,150 --> 00:27:20,942
Parameters, gradients, and optimizer states

661
00:27:20,942 --> 00:27:22,740
are all sharded across the different GPUs.

662
00:27:22,740 --> 00:27:23,640
That's right.

663
00:27:23,640 --> 00:27:24,780
Yeah, good.

664
00:27:24,780 --> 00:27:25,880
Oh, yes.

665
00:27:25,880 --> 00:27:28,280
Why does the communication cost not

666
00:27:28,280 --> 00:27:31,220
multiply by the number of layers as well?

667
00:27:34,280 --> 00:27:37,480
So the number of operations multiplies by layer.

668
00:27:37,480 --> 00:27:38,460
So that's correct.

669
00:27:38,460 --> 00:27:41,340
But each of these communication pieces is smaller, right.

670
00:27:41,340 --> 00:27:44,040
Because this is just one tiny MLP or something.

671
00:27:44,040 --> 00:27:46,320
And I'm comparing that to the cost of all-reducing

672
00:27:46,320 --> 00:27:47,220
a whole network.

673
00:27:47,220 --> 00:27:49,920
Right, so it all adds up in some sense.

674
00:27:49,920 --> 00:27:51,880
Yeah, good.

675
00:27:51,880 --> 00:27:53,680
All right.

676
00:27:53,680 --> 00:27:55,080
OK, good.

677
00:27:55,080 --> 00:27:58,680
So in practice, if you look at the maximum size that can fit

678
00:27:58,680 --> 00:28:04,220
on, let's say, an A100 GPU, if you go from the baseline,

679
00:28:04,220 --> 00:28:06,240
you can not even fit a 7B model.

680
00:28:06,240 --> 00:28:08,120
And you go to ZeRO stage 3.

681
00:28:08,120 --> 00:28:12,660
You can now fit 50 billion parameter models and so on, right.

682
00:28:12,660 --> 00:28:17,260
So it becomes a lot easier to fit these big models using FSDP.

683
00:28:17,260 --> 00:28:19,480
Right, the improvements that you get

684
00:28:19,480 --> 00:28:24,830
in terms of the memory improvement is quite significant.

685
00:28:24,830 --> 00:28:26,790
OK, yes.

686
00:28:26,790 --> 00:28:29,790
So can you go back to the previous slide?

687
00:28:29,790 --> 00:28:39,470
So regarding this on 8x1 A100, what would be the limitation H100 or P200?

688
00:28:39,470 --> 00:28:43,550
OK, I can't do the mental math of multiply by 141

689
00:28:43,550 --> 00:28:47,372
and divide by 80, but that would be the number for an H200.

690
00:28:47,372 --> 00:28:48,330
And so on and so forth.

691
00:28:48,330 --> 00:28:49,550
Yeah, it would be linear, right.

692
00:28:49,550 --> 00:28:51,425
It wouldn't really change the math very much,

693
00:28:51,425 --> 00:28:53,830
though, to go from one accelerator to up.

694
00:28:53,830 --> 00:28:56,570
OK, so FSDP, I think, is very clean.

695
00:28:56,570 --> 00:28:57,410
It's very elegant.

696
00:28:57,410 --> 00:28:58,990
I like it a lot.

697
00:28:58,990 --> 00:29:00,850
I wish I could end the lecture here.

698
00:29:00,850 --> 00:29:04,110
But unfortunately, we will have to proceed into uglier

699
00:29:04,110 --> 00:29:05,910
and more hairy things.

700
00:29:05,910 --> 00:29:08,730
And there's two reasons why we have to do that.

701
00:29:08,730 --> 00:29:10,550
One of them, and this is an important idea

702
00:29:10,550 --> 00:29:13,110
for parallelization, is that in some sense,

703
00:29:13,110 --> 00:29:15,610
data parallel is consuming an important resource.

704
00:29:15,610 --> 00:29:19,090
And that resource is the batch size, so to speak, right.

705
00:29:19,090 --> 00:29:20,870
If you have a batch size of 8, you

706
00:29:20,870 --> 00:29:25,070
can never have more than eight accelerators, right.

707
00:29:25,070 --> 00:29:26,990
And you might think, OK, let's just make

708
00:29:26,990 --> 00:29:28,782
the batch size bigger and bigger and bigger

709
00:29:28,782 --> 00:29:30,390
as we have accelerators.

710
00:29:30,390 --> 00:29:33,510
You can't do that, because at a certain point,

711
00:29:33,510 --> 00:29:35,830
there's something called the critical batch size, where

712
00:29:35,830 --> 00:29:39,150
the basically gain that you get from the additional batch

713
00:29:39,150 --> 00:29:42,510
element is less than if you had taken another SGD step

714
00:29:42,510 --> 00:29:43,950
on that single element, right.

715
00:29:43,950 --> 00:29:46,930
So there comes a point where at small batch sizes,

716
00:29:46,930 --> 00:29:49,570
adding extra elements is perfectly helpful.

717
00:29:49,570 --> 00:29:51,225
It's the same as taking more SGD steps.

718
00:29:51,225 --> 00:29:53,350
But at a certain point, diminishing return hits in.

719
00:29:53,350 --> 00:29:55,945
An infinitely large batch size is not infinitely better

720
00:29:55,945 --> 00:29:57,070
than infinite steps, right.

721
00:29:57,070 --> 00:29:59,310
Infinite single steps.

722
00:29:59,310 --> 00:30:02,370
Because of this, now we have faced a hard tradeoff.

723
00:30:02,370 --> 00:30:06,110
Do we want small batch sizes and let our GPUs be more idle?

724
00:30:06,110 --> 00:30:09,870
Do we want big batch sizes and take the hit from optimization?

725
00:30:09,870 --> 00:30:11,430
Hard to say, right.

726
00:30:11,430 --> 00:30:13,290
There's another issue that we have,

727
00:30:13,290 --> 00:30:16,230
which is that we still have memory issues, which

728
00:30:16,230 --> 00:30:19,190
is ZeRO stages 1 and 2 don't really

729
00:30:19,190 --> 00:30:21,170
let you scale memory at all.

730
00:30:21,170 --> 00:30:25,270
And ZeRO stage 2 lets you cut up the parameter memory.

731
00:30:25,270 --> 00:30:27,990
But unfortunately, there's activation memory

732
00:30:27,990 --> 00:30:31,510
and other kinds of memory use that this does not split up

733
00:30:31,510 --> 00:30:33,710
or this does not reduce at all, right.

734
00:30:33,710 --> 00:30:36,270
And so we need better ways, more fine grained ways,

735
00:30:36,270 --> 00:30:38,590
of cutting up the model in order to really start

736
00:30:38,590 --> 00:30:41,290
pushing down the memory use beyond these points, right.

737
00:30:41,290 --> 00:30:48,270
And so this takes us to various model parallelism ideas.

738
00:30:48,270 --> 00:30:49,990
And so model parallelism is going

739
00:30:49,990 --> 00:30:53,910
to be this idea of essentially like FSDP,

740
00:30:53,910 --> 00:30:57,070
we're going to split up the parameters across GPUs.

741
00:30:57,070 --> 00:30:59,430
But I think the important conceptual difference

742
00:30:59,430 --> 00:31:02,990
between what we talked about and what we will talk about next

743
00:31:02,990 --> 00:31:05,550
is that now we are going to communicate activations.

744
00:31:05,550 --> 00:31:07,910
Right, in FSDP, we cut up the parameters.

745
00:31:07,910 --> 00:31:10,250
But in some sense, it was just a wrapper.

746
00:31:10,250 --> 00:31:12,365
We were still doing the normal computation.

747
00:31:12,365 --> 00:31:14,990
We were just sending parameters back and forth before doing it.

748
00:31:14,990 --> 00:31:17,373
Right, so parameters were the things flying around.

749
00:31:17,373 --> 00:31:18,790
Now what we're going to do instead

750
00:31:18,790 --> 00:31:20,550
is we're going to communicate activations

751
00:31:20,550 --> 00:31:21,510
back and forth, right.

752
00:31:21,510 --> 00:31:24,530
So if, for example, one layer lives on GPU 0,

753
00:31:24,530 --> 00:31:26,295
the next one lives on GPU 1.

754
00:31:26,295 --> 00:31:27,670
Right, what I have to communicate

755
00:31:27,670 --> 00:31:29,850
is the activation of the layer in between.

756
00:31:29,850 --> 00:31:32,370
Right, so that's going to be the big difference.

757
00:31:32,370 --> 00:31:36,110
And this will involve pipeline parallel, which is cutting up

758
00:31:36,110 --> 00:31:39,450
layers, tensor parallel, which is cutting up matrices,

759
00:31:39,450 --> 00:31:41,630
and expert parallel, which is sharding experts

760
00:31:41,630 --> 00:31:42,810
into different places.

761
00:31:42,810 --> 00:31:45,690
Right, and Percy's talked about tensor parallel already,

762
00:31:45,690 --> 00:31:49,390
so you have roughly some mental model of this.

763
00:31:49,390 --> 00:31:52,390
I'll start by talking about pipeline parallel or layer

764
00:31:52,390 --> 00:31:54,470
wise parallel.

765
00:31:54,470 --> 00:31:56,450
This is, I think, conceptually very simple.

766
00:31:56,450 --> 00:31:59,715
Back in the days when I first learned about parallelism,

767
00:31:59,715 --> 00:32:01,090
I was like, ah, yes, it's simple.

768
00:32:01,090 --> 00:32:03,340
Just cut up the layers and put them on different GPUs.

769
00:32:03,340 --> 00:32:05,270
Right, very simple thing.

770
00:32:05,270 --> 00:32:07,570
And so you're going to pass activations forward

771
00:32:07,570 --> 00:32:09,070
in the forward pass, and then you're

772
00:32:09,070 --> 00:32:10,630
going to pass like partial gradients

773
00:32:10,630 --> 00:32:13,230
backwards in the backward pass.

774
00:32:13,230 --> 00:32:15,690
Now, OK, if I do this, what will happen?

775
00:32:15,690 --> 00:32:18,310
You will get a terrible looking picture like this.

776
00:32:18,310 --> 00:32:19,250
Very, very depressing.

777
00:32:19,250 --> 00:32:23,880
I have four accelerators, each handling a quarter of the model.

778
00:32:23,880 --> 00:32:26,130
And so what computation happens as a function of time.

779
00:32:26,130 --> 00:32:28,090
So time is sweeping from left to right.

780
00:32:28,090 --> 00:32:30,990
And the different accelerators are the different rows.

781
00:32:30,990 --> 00:32:34,910
So first, my zeroth accelerator at the bottom here,

782
00:32:34,910 --> 00:32:37,602
that's handling the first layer will do some computation.

783
00:32:37,602 --> 00:32:38,810
And then it will stop, right.

784
00:32:38,810 --> 00:32:41,370
And then it will hand off the computation to the second one.

785
00:32:41,370 --> 00:32:43,830
And then it will do nothing for the rest of time

786
00:32:43,830 --> 00:32:44,672
until the very end.

787
00:32:44,672 --> 00:32:47,130
And then I will basically be passing things back and forth.

788
00:32:47,130 --> 00:32:49,390
And one GPU will be active at a time.

789
00:32:49,390 --> 00:32:51,430
And then coming backwards, one GPU

790
00:32:51,430 --> 00:32:54,590
will be active handling the backward pass at a time.

791
00:32:54,590 --> 00:32:57,170
And so most of the time, my GPUs are idle.

792
00:32:57,170 --> 00:33:00,950
My utilization is truly, truly terrible.

793
00:33:00,950 --> 00:33:02,410
And this is called a bubble.

794
00:33:02,410 --> 00:33:06,150
It's the part of the pipeline where you're not doing anything.

795
00:33:06,150 --> 00:33:09,750
And the solution to this is to essentially do

796
00:33:09,750 --> 00:33:11,830
a batching or pipelining.

797
00:33:11,830 --> 00:33:15,005
So instead of doing a simple one layer after another,

798
00:33:15,005 --> 00:33:16,630
you're going to have a pipeline, right.

799
00:33:16,630 --> 00:33:18,890
Where, in this case, in this picture here,

800
00:33:18,890 --> 00:33:20,730
you have four elements to be processed.

801
00:33:20,730 --> 00:33:24,430
And as soon as you process one, you start on the next element,

802
00:33:24,430 --> 00:33:26,310
and then you pass off the one that's already

803
00:33:26,310 --> 00:33:27,510
done to your next layer.

804
00:33:27,510 --> 00:33:30,630
Right, so you can do this to essentially pass elements

805
00:33:30,630 --> 00:33:33,390
upwards and then back downwards.

806
00:33:33,390 --> 00:33:37,510
And so if you want to make this reasonable, then what you need

807
00:33:37,510 --> 00:33:40,470
is the ratio of essentially this bubble

808
00:33:40,470 --> 00:33:41,610
time to the useful compute.

809
00:33:41,610 --> 00:33:43,310
The utilization, in some sense, is

810
00:33:43,310 --> 00:33:47,770
going to be the number of stages divided by this micro-batches.

811
00:33:47,770 --> 00:33:50,670
And so we need a huge batch size in order

812
00:33:50,670 --> 00:33:52,850
to reduce this bubble towards 0.

813
00:33:52,850 --> 00:33:55,210
Right, so if we want our bubble to be 0,

814
00:33:55,210 --> 00:33:59,430
it's going to go down as 1 over the microbatch size.

815
00:33:59,430 --> 00:34:03,170
This is why I said batch sizes are useful resource.

816
00:34:03,170 --> 00:34:05,590
Because now we can spend it in a different way, which

817
00:34:05,590 --> 00:34:10,230
is to pipeline more to reduce the amount of idle time

818
00:34:10,230 --> 00:34:13,219
in pipeline parallel.

819
00:34:13,219 --> 00:34:15,620
OK, so pipelines are terrible.

820
00:34:15,620 --> 00:34:17,420
And by folklore, people say things

821
00:34:17,420 --> 00:34:20,237
like, parallelization code looks reasonable

822
00:34:20,237 --> 00:34:21,820
and people can understand it until you

823
00:34:21,820 --> 00:34:23,980
implement pipeline parallel.

824
00:34:23,980 --> 00:34:25,840
So why do we do pipeline parallel?

825
00:34:25,840 --> 00:34:28,420
Well, pipelines can save memory compared to DDP.

826
00:34:28,420 --> 00:34:30,780
Because we've cut up layers, and you can also compose

827
00:34:30,780 --> 00:34:31,880
this with data parallel.

828
00:34:31,880 --> 00:34:33,739
So you can do both.

829
00:34:33,739 --> 00:34:37,340
But the other reason why this is a very important idea to learn,

830
00:34:37,340 --> 00:34:39,699
and you will always have a pipeline in certain kinds

831
00:34:39,699 --> 00:34:42,179
of topologies, is that it has very good communication

832
00:34:42,179 --> 00:34:45,699
properties compared to both FSDP and compared to any other kinds

833
00:34:45,699 --> 00:34:47,300
of things you can do.

834
00:34:47,300 --> 00:34:51,040
It only depends on activations, like b times s times h.

835
00:34:51,040 --> 00:34:52,405
It is also point to point.

836
00:34:52,405 --> 00:34:54,280
One layer is going to communicate to another.

837
00:34:54,280 --> 00:34:55,620
It's not all to all.

838
00:34:55,620 --> 00:34:58,360
And so depending on how you sequence these things,

839
00:34:58,360 --> 00:35:01,980
they can be a very efficient way to utilize your network.

840
00:35:01,980 --> 00:35:03,900
So in practice, what we're going to do

841
00:35:03,900 --> 00:35:06,820
is that pipelines are going to live on the slowest networking

842
00:35:06,820 --> 00:35:07,320
links.

843
00:35:07,320 --> 00:35:11,100
So if you have multiple data centers or multiple pods that

844
00:35:11,100 --> 00:35:12,980
are slower to talk to, you're going

845
00:35:12,980 --> 00:35:15,920
to parallelize across those two using pipeline parallel,

846
00:35:15,920 --> 00:35:17,580
because that's the most communication

847
00:35:17,580 --> 00:35:22,760
efficient parallelism that you can bring to bear here.

848
00:35:22,760 --> 00:35:23,260
OK.

849
00:35:23,260 --> 00:35:26,780
And as I said, pipeline performance

850
00:35:26,780 --> 00:35:28,600
is really dependent on batch size.

851
00:35:28,600 --> 00:35:30,662
So I'll point out this paper repeatedly,

852
00:35:30,662 --> 00:35:32,120
but I'll mention which one this is.

853
00:35:32,120 --> 00:35:35,200
Now this is the Megatron paper from NVIDIA.

854
00:35:35,200 --> 00:35:38,780
They have a bunch of really lovely parameter sweeps of what

855
00:35:38,780 --> 00:35:41,300
happens to utilization as a function

856
00:35:41,300 --> 00:35:44,620
of different configurations you can do.

857
00:35:44,620 --> 00:35:48,500
So if you have large batch sizes and you pick a large pipeline

858
00:35:48,500 --> 00:35:50,700
size, you can get pretty good utilization

859
00:35:50,700 --> 00:35:54,060
close to not doing pipeline parallel at all.

860
00:35:54,060 --> 00:35:56,300
But you need the big batch sizes in order

861
00:35:56,300 --> 00:35:58,358
to be able to keep utilization up.

862
00:35:58,358 --> 00:35:59,900
Otherwise, pipeline parallel is going

863
00:35:59,900 --> 00:36:03,472
to rapidly degrade your utilization and performance.

864
00:36:03,472 --> 00:36:05,180
Actually, I'm going to pause for a moment

865
00:36:05,180 --> 00:36:08,620
here to ask questions, because the next slide is complicated.

866
00:36:08,620 --> 00:36:09,560
Yes?

867
00:36:09,560 --> 00:36:12,020
So you mentioned on the previous slide

868
00:36:12,020 --> 00:36:14,720
that pipelines have good communication properties,

869
00:36:14,720 --> 00:36:16,220
like the FSPD.

870
00:36:16,220 --> 00:36:19,740
What is the specific mechanism for why that is?

871
00:36:19,740 --> 00:36:22,400
It's a smaller amount of stuff that's being communicated.

872
00:36:22,400 --> 00:36:25,720
So it's batch times sequence length times hidden.

873
00:36:25,720 --> 00:36:27,700
And that's almost always a smaller amount

874
00:36:27,700 --> 00:36:30,700
of data than attempting to communicate a whole parameter

875
00:36:30,700 --> 00:36:32,820
matrix, right.

876
00:36:35,380 --> 00:36:38,140
So people have tried to figure out

877
00:36:38,140 --> 00:36:41,780
much more clever ways of reducing

878
00:36:41,780 --> 00:36:43,760
the amount of pipeline sizes.

879
00:36:43,760 --> 00:36:46,900
And you can do clever things that

880
00:36:46,900 --> 00:36:48,940
cut up the different stages of what

881
00:36:48,940 --> 00:36:52,600
you're doing for different layers or different micro-batches.

882
00:36:52,600 --> 00:36:55,260
So you can sequence different forward pass elements

883
00:36:55,260 --> 00:36:57,740
in between different backwards pass elements.

884
00:36:57,740 --> 00:36:59,700
And by doing clever scheduling, you

885
00:36:59,700 --> 00:37:01,920
can further reduce the bubble size.

886
00:37:01,920 --> 00:37:03,600
This was taken from the DeepSeek paper.

887
00:37:03,600 --> 00:37:05,700
So by really cleverly manipulating

888
00:37:05,700 --> 00:37:07,880
when you're doing forward and backward computation,

889
00:37:07,880 --> 00:37:13,100
you can get a little bit better in terms of the bubble size.

890
00:37:13,100 --> 00:37:16,060
But I think the even more clever thing that you can do,

891
00:37:16,060 --> 00:37:19,460
which is called zero bubble pipelining,

892
00:37:19,460 --> 00:37:22,380
is not really about scheduling per se.

893
00:37:22,380 --> 00:37:25,060
But it's actually about thinking carefully

894
00:37:25,060 --> 00:37:28,220
about the structure of your backward pass.

895
00:37:28,220 --> 00:37:30,100
So in the backward pass, there's really

896
00:37:30,100 --> 00:37:32,180
two things that have to happen.

897
00:37:32,180 --> 00:37:34,340
If you think about it, you're going backwards

898
00:37:34,340 --> 00:37:35,437
in your computation graph.

899
00:37:35,437 --> 00:37:37,520
And as you do that, you're going to do two things.

900
00:37:37,520 --> 00:37:39,380
One is going to be you're going to propagate

901
00:37:39,380 --> 00:37:42,480
your partial derivatives further down your computation graph.

902
00:37:42,480 --> 00:37:45,220
So remember, back propagation, you start at the end,

903
00:37:45,220 --> 00:37:47,800
and you're propagating these partial derivatives back, right.

904
00:37:47,800 --> 00:37:49,110
So you have to do that.

905
00:37:49,110 --> 00:37:50,860
But you also want to compute the gradients

906
00:37:50,860 --> 00:37:52,080
for the current weight.

907
00:37:52,080 --> 00:37:53,663
Right, so you have two things that you

908
00:37:53,663 --> 00:37:55,480
do at every node of your computation graph.

909
00:37:55,480 --> 00:37:57,100
You're going to propagate backwards,

910
00:37:57,100 --> 00:38:00,140
and you're going to compute the derivative of your weight.

911
00:38:00,140 --> 00:38:02,260
The important thing about thinking

912
00:38:02,260 --> 00:38:06,220
about this compute graph is actually, one of these two

913
00:38:06,220 --> 00:38:08,740
is really important for pipelines.

914
00:38:08,740 --> 00:38:11,460
And the other one, you can do whenever.

915
00:38:11,460 --> 00:38:13,080
Propagating the partials backwards,

916
00:38:13,080 --> 00:38:14,340
right, that's very important.

917
00:38:14,340 --> 00:38:17,500
Because your next stage can't do any work until you've propagated

918
00:38:17,500 --> 00:38:18,605
that signal back.

919
00:38:18,605 --> 00:38:19,980
On the other hand, the derivative

920
00:38:19,980 --> 00:38:21,620
with respect to the weights, that

921
00:38:21,620 --> 00:38:23,827
is a leaf node, so to speak, on this graph.

922
00:38:23,827 --> 00:38:25,160
And I can do it whenever, right.

923
00:38:25,160 --> 00:38:29,400
And so the clever thing to do now is you separate these two.

924
00:38:29,400 --> 00:38:31,320
Right, so you got your B's over here.

925
00:38:31,320 --> 00:38:35,120
These B's are the backwards, propagating backwards.

926
00:38:35,120 --> 00:38:37,340
And then these W's, these W's are computing

927
00:38:37,340 --> 00:38:38,640
the derivative of the weights.

928
00:38:38,640 --> 00:38:40,140
And they can happen whenever.

929
00:38:40,140 --> 00:38:44,000
So what you do is you compute the B's as quickly as you can.

930
00:38:44,000 --> 00:38:45,400
Right, so those come first.

931
00:38:45,400 --> 00:38:47,460
And then you can defer the W's until later

932
00:38:47,460 --> 00:38:50,360
and do it when you have a gap in your computation.

933
00:38:50,360 --> 00:38:53,620
And when you do this, you can basically almost all completely

934
00:38:53,620 --> 00:38:56,180
fill up the pipeline.

935
00:38:56,180 --> 00:38:58,180
And the zero bubble pipelining thing

936
00:38:58,180 --> 00:39:01,220
is very complicated or much more complicated

937
00:39:01,220 --> 00:39:03,000
than you would normally like to deal with.

938
00:39:03,000 --> 00:39:06,060
But it allows you to almost deal with these pipelining issues

939
00:39:06,060 --> 00:39:08,740
almost entirely, depending on essentially the workload

940
00:39:08,740 --> 00:39:12,900
involved in the W versus the B, and so on and so forth.

941
00:39:12,900 --> 00:39:16,280
But I think having seen this, I was like, oh, this is so clever.

942
00:39:16,280 --> 00:39:19,460
Such clever things that you can do with systems.

943
00:39:19,460 --> 00:39:22,820
OK, so that was pipelining.

944
00:39:22,820 --> 00:39:26,220
Any questions about that?

945
00:39:26,220 --> 00:39:27,740
Good.

946
00:39:27,740 --> 00:39:30,980
So I think there's a natural analogy

947
00:39:30,980 --> 00:39:32,640
or a parallel you can think of.

948
00:39:32,640 --> 00:39:34,520
So pipeline parallel is like depth.

949
00:39:34,520 --> 00:39:36,660
So your depth, you cut up your network

950
00:39:36,660 --> 00:39:38,620
along the depth dimension.

951
00:39:38,620 --> 00:39:40,560
But we have two ways that we scale, right.

952
00:39:40,560 --> 00:39:43,220
We've got a depth axis and we have a width axis.

953
00:39:43,220 --> 00:39:46,400
So what if we start cutting up our model along width, right?

954
00:39:46,400 --> 00:39:50,580
So this is tensor parallel, as Percy explained before.

955
00:39:50,580 --> 00:39:53,380
And it's really the simple idea that matrix multiplies

956
00:39:53,380 --> 00:39:56,580
can be cut up into smaller matrix multiplies, which

957
00:39:56,580 --> 00:39:58,960
you then add the partial sums back together.

958
00:39:58,960 --> 00:40:01,520
This is really the same idea as tiling as well.

959
00:40:01,520 --> 00:40:07,660
So this core primitive just appears many, many times.

960
00:40:07,660 --> 00:40:09,940
And so when you do this in practice,

961
00:40:09,940 --> 00:40:12,260
right, what does this actually look like?

962
00:40:12,260 --> 00:40:14,715
So this is a GeLU that's happening.

963
00:40:14,715 --> 00:40:15,840
So what do you normally do?

964
00:40:15,840 --> 00:40:19,020
You have your x coming in as input on the very right

965
00:40:19,020 --> 00:40:20,620
of this slide here.

966
00:40:20,620 --> 00:40:23,240
And then I have a matrix A that I multiply with.

967
00:40:23,240 --> 00:40:25,420
I apply my non-linearity GeLU.

968
00:40:25,420 --> 00:40:28,240
And then I multiply with B to downproject.

969
00:40:28,240 --> 00:40:29,580
And then I output my z.

970
00:40:29,580 --> 00:40:33,140
Right, this is the normal serial computation that I do.

971
00:40:33,140 --> 00:40:34,980
Now in tensor parallel, what I would do

972
00:40:34,980 --> 00:40:37,940
is I would cut out my A into two submatrices, my B

973
00:40:37,940 --> 00:40:41,380
into two submatrices, and then I would run the computations

974
00:40:41,380 --> 00:40:45,180
in parallel, and I would gather them together at the end.

975
00:40:45,180 --> 00:40:48,780
Now, one thing that is important is

976
00:40:48,780 --> 00:40:51,560
there's a duality between the forward and backward pass.

977
00:40:51,560 --> 00:40:56,095
So in the forward pass, all I do with the x is I copy them twice,

978
00:40:56,095 --> 00:40:57,720
and then I run the computation forward.

979
00:40:57,720 --> 00:41:00,420
So f is the identity, this f function.

980
00:41:00,420 --> 00:41:03,730
And then once I get to the end of my parallel component,

981
00:41:03,730 --> 00:41:06,410
the g function, that's an all-reduce that brings them

982
00:41:06,410 --> 00:41:07,570
back together, right.

983
00:41:07,570 --> 00:41:10,050
But in the backwards pass, as I'm doing my gradients,

984
00:41:10,050 --> 00:41:11,210
this is going to flip.

985
00:41:11,210 --> 00:41:14,147
My g is going to be on-- sorry, my g is going to be an identity.

986
00:41:14,147 --> 00:41:17,250
Right, because the partial derivatives are coming backwards.

987
00:41:17,250 --> 00:41:19,730
And then my f are the partials that I

988
00:41:19,730 --> 00:41:23,130
need to sum up in order to get my partial derivatives

989
00:41:23,130 --> 00:41:24,737
at the start of this module.

990
00:41:24,737 --> 00:41:26,070
Right, so they're going to flip.

991
00:41:26,070 --> 00:41:28,250
And this duality is important if you're

992
00:41:28,250 --> 00:41:31,970
going to write tensor parallel.

993
00:41:31,970 --> 00:41:33,290
OK.

994
00:41:33,290 --> 00:41:36,470
And so you might have already noticed, right.

995
00:41:36,470 --> 00:41:39,450
But when we split things, some of the ways

996
00:41:39,450 --> 00:41:41,150
that we are going to be column-wise.

997
00:41:41,150 --> 00:41:43,330
So column-wise, tensor parallel is

998
00:41:43,330 --> 00:41:44,990
going to happen at the inputs.

999
00:41:44,990 --> 00:41:48,450
Right, so the inputs of the MLP, the projections of the attention

1000
00:41:48,450 --> 00:41:52,650
are going to be cut up by the columns in each transformer

1001
00:41:52,650 --> 00:41:53,150
block.

1002
00:41:53,150 --> 00:41:55,770
So this is column-wise cuts.

1003
00:41:55,770 --> 00:41:57,890
And then there's going to be row-wise cuts

1004
00:41:57,890 --> 00:42:00,150
in the corresponding second stage.

1005
00:42:00,150 --> 00:42:03,330
So in the downprojections of the MLP,

1006
00:42:03,330 --> 00:42:05,030
as well as the outputs of the attention,

1007
00:42:05,030 --> 00:42:06,650
you're going to have a row-wise cut.

1008
00:42:06,650 --> 00:42:09,850
And then everything that's a small layer, like a layer

1009
00:42:09,850 --> 00:42:12,990
norm or a non-linearity or routers in ML--

1010
00:42:12,990 --> 00:42:15,850
sorry, in MoEs, those are all fully replicated

1011
00:42:15,850 --> 00:42:16,710
across the machines.

1012
00:42:16,710 --> 00:42:19,930
You don't really want to bother with the overhead of cutting

1013
00:42:19,930 --> 00:42:22,030
those guys.

1014
00:42:22,030 --> 00:42:22,530
OK.

1015
00:42:22,530 --> 00:42:26,210
And then, so when do we do tensor parallel?

1016
00:42:26,210 --> 00:42:31,450
Tensor parallel is extremely computation hungry.

1017
00:42:31,450 --> 00:42:34,017
What you're doing is every time you have a matmul,

1018
00:42:34,017 --> 00:42:35,850
you're going to be doing some communication.

1019
00:42:35,850 --> 00:42:37,170
Right, you have an all-reduce.

1020
00:42:37,170 --> 00:42:41,090
Or in the backwards pass, you have all-reduce here.

1021
00:42:41,090 --> 00:42:44,330
One in the G when you're going forward.

1022
00:42:44,330 --> 00:42:46,130
And these are activation size, and you're

1023
00:42:46,130 --> 00:42:48,170
doing them very frequently.

1024
00:42:48,170 --> 00:42:50,710
So this is very communication hungry.

1025
00:42:50,710 --> 00:42:54,710
And so you only want to do this within a node.

1026
00:42:54,710 --> 00:42:58,970
So for GPUs, GPUs are going to be networked tightly together

1027
00:42:58,970 --> 00:43:00,690
in a single box.

1028
00:43:00,690 --> 00:43:03,370
And so up to eight, you might be willing to do some tensor

1029
00:43:03,370 --> 00:43:03,990
parallel.

1030
00:43:03,990 --> 00:43:07,150
Once you go beyond that, you're going to be going cross node.

1031
00:43:07,150 --> 00:43:10,590
And those interconnects, those connections, are much slower.

1032
00:43:10,590 --> 00:43:12,810
And so you're going to get a big drop in performance

1033
00:43:12,810 --> 00:43:16,710
as soon as you hit that point of going beyond a single machine.

1034
00:43:16,710 --> 00:43:20,130
So tensor parallel, you should remember as very communication

1035
00:43:20,130 --> 00:43:20,790
hungry.

1036
00:43:20,790 --> 00:43:23,170
You usually use it in the fastest interconnects

1037
00:43:23,170 --> 00:43:26,410
that you have at hand.

1038
00:43:26,410 --> 00:43:28,530
And this is the point at which I'm

1039
00:43:28,530 --> 00:43:31,150
going to go back to the thing I said about TPUs.

1040
00:43:31,150 --> 00:43:33,410
Right, remember for TPUs, you don't really

1041
00:43:33,410 --> 00:43:36,330
have this distinction between fast eight GPUs

1042
00:43:36,330 --> 00:43:37,470
and next machines.

1043
00:43:37,470 --> 00:43:39,130
You just have this big mesh.

1044
00:43:39,130 --> 00:43:41,650
And so one of the big advantages that TPU people will

1045
00:43:41,650 --> 00:43:45,410
tell you is you can tensor parallel very large numbers

1046
00:43:45,410 --> 00:43:47,810
compared to the GPU world, because they're

1047
00:43:47,810 --> 00:43:51,330
able to have high bandwidth in this very regular communication

1048
00:43:51,330 --> 00:43:53,310
pattern over this mesh.

1049
00:43:53,310 --> 00:43:54,730
Right, so that's a big difference

1050
00:43:54,730 --> 00:43:57,470
if you're parallelizing between TPUs versus GPUs.

1051
00:43:57,470 --> 00:44:00,610
The amount of tensor parallelism you use versus pipeline parallel

1052
00:44:00,610 --> 00:44:02,850
will actually be quite different.

1053
00:44:02,850 --> 00:44:05,430
So how do we compare these two.

1054
00:44:05,430 --> 00:44:07,310
They're both model splitting strategies.

1055
00:44:07,310 --> 00:44:10,090
They'll both save on both parameters

1056
00:44:10,090 --> 00:44:12,610
and potentially activations.

1057
00:44:12,610 --> 00:44:15,010
The pros here of tensor parallel is that there's

1058
00:44:15,010 --> 00:44:17,370
no necessarily pipeline bubble.

1059
00:44:17,370 --> 00:44:19,250
And if your network's fast enough,

1060
00:44:19,250 --> 00:44:21,770
you might be able to get full utilization.

1061
00:44:21,770 --> 00:44:23,070
It's also low complexity.

1062
00:44:23,070 --> 00:44:25,410
It's just cutting up some matmuls.

1063
00:44:25,410 --> 00:44:28,790
But the con is that the communication is much larger.

1064
00:44:28,790 --> 00:44:31,530
It used to be that you were doing b times s times h

1065
00:44:31,530 --> 00:44:33,110
point to point communication.

1066
00:44:33,110 --> 00:44:35,690
Now instead, I'm going to be doing something like a times

1067
00:44:35,690 --> 00:44:40,730
b times s times h times roughly one all-reduce communication,

1068
00:44:40,730 --> 00:44:41,230
right.

1069
00:44:41,230 --> 00:44:42,897
So this is not even just point to point.

1070
00:44:42,897 --> 00:44:46,010
This is all to all communication that needs to happen.

1071
00:44:46,010 --> 00:44:47,810
So tensor parallel is great.

1072
00:44:47,810 --> 00:44:50,690
Whenever we have high speed interconnects, every other time,

1073
00:44:50,690 --> 00:44:53,490
we probably want to be using pipeline parallel.

1074
00:44:53,490 --> 00:44:55,762
OK.

1075
00:44:55,762 --> 00:44:58,490
So the last thing I want to talk about about--

1076
00:44:58,490 --> 00:45:03,170
we're talking about memory use, is to really think

1077
00:45:03,170 --> 00:45:04,730
more carefully about memory.

1078
00:45:04,730 --> 00:45:08,010
I think the most naive view of memory in deep learning

1079
00:45:08,010 --> 00:45:10,730
and language modeling is something like, well, memory

1080
00:45:10,730 --> 00:45:11,870
is just parameters.

1081
00:45:11,870 --> 00:45:13,328
That's this green bit over here.

1082
00:45:13,328 --> 00:45:15,370
Right, you have a certain number of bits you need

1083
00:45:15,370 --> 00:45:16,770
to store for your parameters.

1084
00:45:16,770 --> 00:45:19,000
That's it.

1085
00:45:19,000 --> 00:45:20,750
I've already talked about optimizer state,

1086
00:45:20,750 --> 00:45:23,713
which is important, which is this yellow box over here.

1087
00:45:23,713 --> 00:45:30,010
But if you look at this plot, this actual memory use as profiled in one of the PyTorch profilers.

1088
00:45:30,010 --> 00:45:33,470
You'll find actually that this is not really the whole story.

1089
00:45:33,470 --> 00:45:35,730
There's a lot of dynamic memory that needs

1090
00:45:35,730 --> 00:45:38,367
to exist in a computation.

1091
00:45:38,367 --> 00:45:39,950
You need to store all the activations.

1092
00:45:39,950 --> 00:45:41,890
These are these big red bumps over here.

1093
00:45:41,890 --> 00:45:43,590
And then as you compute your gradients,

1094
00:45:43,590 --> 00:45:46,250
you need to store them in your backward sweep, right.

1095
00:45:46,250 --> 00:45:48,930
And so really maximum memory usage

1096
00:45:48,930 --> 00:45:52,350
happens a little bit after the maximum activation point.

1097
00:45:52,350 --> 00:45:55,030
After you start sweeping backwards on your gradient,

1098
00:45:55,030 --> 00:45:57,870
but you still need to compute a lot-- keep your activations,

1099
00:45:57,870 --> 00:46:00,070
those are usually the maximum memory points, right.

1100
00:46:00,070 --> 00:46:03,470
So to reduce this, maybe we need to deal with this big red hump,

1101
00:46:03,470 --> 00:46:06,290
this activation.

1102
00:46:06,290 --> 00:46:12,090
And if you start looking at more modern workloads for language

1103
00:46:12,090 --> 00:46:14,050
models, you start to find that as you

1104
00:46:14,050 --> 00:46:16,242
get to bigger and bigger parameters--

1105
00:46:16,242 --> 00:46:17,950
forget the column that says present work.

1106
00:46:17,950 --> 00:46:19,630
I'll talk about that in a moment here.

1107
00:46:19,630 --> 00:46:22,150
But if you look at just the baseline columns of this plot,

1108
00:46:22,150 --> 00:46:24,890
as you go to bigger models, for moderately large sequence

1109
00:46:24,890 --> 00:46:27,370
lengths, your activations are just

1110
00:46:27,370 --> 00:46:30,690
going to dwarf the parameter memory that you need.

1111
00:46:30,690 --> 00:46:33,850
And so any memory saving strategy

1112
00:46:33,850 --> 00:46:37,910
has to reason about activations in order to be fully effective.

1113
00:46:37,910 --> 00:46:40,670
We can't just deal with parameter memory.

1114
00:46:40,670 --> 00:46:44,570
That's not really sufficient for us.

1115
00:46:44,570 --> 00:46:47,590
So if we're storing everything-- and this is a good,

1116
00:46:47,590 --> 00:46:51,650
I don't know, statistic or rule of thumb to start with.

1117
00:46:51,650 --> 00:46:53,490
If we need to store everything, the amount

1118
00:46:53,490 --> 00:46:56,240
of activations that we need is going

1119
00:46:56,240 --> 00:47:00,100
to be something like 34 times s times b times h,

1120
00:47:00,100 --> 00:47:03,080
and that's going to be sequence length times batch

1121
00:47:03,080 --> 00:47:08,640
size times hidden dimension size plus 5 times as over h.

1122
00:47:08,640 --> 00:47:12,640
as is attention heads times sequence length divided

1123
00:47:12,640 --> 00:47:14,140
by hidden dimension size.

1124
00:47:14,140 --> 00:47:17,800
And this sbh dependence, right, is fundamental,

1125
00:47:17,800 --> 00:47:20,200
because we expect to have to store something

1126
00:47:20,200 --> 00:47:21,833
for every element of the sequence.

1127
00:47:21,833 --> 00:47:24,000
We expect to have to store something for every batch

1128
00:47:24,000 --> 00:47:26,560
element, and we expect to have to store something

1129
00:47:26,560 --> 00:47:27,880
for every hidden dim size.

1130
00:47:27,880 --> 00:47:30,600
Right, so we're going to see this sbh term repeatedly

1131
00:47:30,600 --> 00:47:33,240
as we try to go through different ways of reducing

1132
00:47:33,240 --> 00:47:35,720
activation memory and accounting for the total amount

1133
00:47:35,720 --> 00:47:36,992
of activation memory.

1134
00:47:36,992 --> 00:47:39,200
And in case you're curious where the terms come from,

1135
00:47:39,200 --> 00:47:42,173
this odd looking term, the 5 as over h

1136
00:47:42,173 --> 00:47:44,340
is going to come from the quadratic attention terms,

1137
00:47:44,340 --> 00:47:46,120
including the dropout terms.

1138
00:47:46,120 --> 00:47:48,240
And we can drop this term via recomputation

1139
00:47:48,240 --> 00:47:49,580
if we do flash attention.

1140
00:47:52,360 --> 00:47:52,960
OK.

1141
00:47:52,960 --> 00:47:55,120
Now what if we did tensor parallel?

1142
00:47:55,120 --> 00:47:57,760
One of the promises that I made to you

1143
00:47:57,760 --> 00:47:59,600
in talking about model parallelism

1144
00:47:59,600 --> 00:48:03,680
was that we could reduce activation quite a bit.

1145
00:48:03,680 --> 00:48:05,620
So now let's think about what happens.

1146
00:48:05,620 --> 00:48:07,620
Well, tensor parallel is going to split out

1147
00:48:07,620 --> 00:48:10,380
the matrix multiplies in attention and MLPs.

1148
00:48:10,380 --> 00:48:11,760
So which parts are those?

1149
00:48:11,760 --> 00:48:18,520
Well, the MLPs are 24 of those 34 that I talked about before.

1150
00:48:18,520 --> 00:48:22,280
And the attention heads are 5 as over ht.

1151
00:48:22,280 --> 00:48:24,280
Right, the second term over here.

1152
00:48:24,280 --> 00:48:27,580
Now, I can reduce that by t, my tensor parallel size.

1153
00:48:27,580 --> 00:48:28,900
And that's good.

1154
00:48:28,900 --> 00:48:30,040
This is not bad.

1155
00:48:30,040 --> 00:48:33,200
Because I've taken most of the memory,

1156
00:48:33,200 --> 00:48:36,820
and I've made it scale linearly with the tensor parallel size t.

1157
00:48:36,820 --> 00:48:38,880
So that's good.

1158
00:48:38,880 --> 00:48:42,080
But it turns out that there are other things that you do.

1159
00:48:42,080 --> 00:48:43,460
You might have a layer norm.

1160
00:48:43,460 --> 00:48:44,840
你可能有一个 dropout。

1161
00:48:44,840 --> 00:48:47,800
你需要保留注意力机制和 MLP 的输入。

1162
00:48:47,800 --> 00:48:49,800
对，因为每一层的输入

1163
00:48:49,800 --> 00:48:53,720
都需要作为残差存储起来，用于反向传播。

1164
00:48:53,720 --> 00:48:56,640
不幸的是，这些并不会被归约

1165
00:48:56,640 --> 00:48:58,060
随着你的张量并行大小。

1166
00:48:58,060 --> 00:49:00,560
对，张量并行不会拆分层归一化。

1167
00:49:00,560 --> 00:49:03,660
因此，它不会拆分这些的激活值。

1168
00:49:03,660 --> 00:49:06,720
所以这很不幸，因为你原本希望如果你有

1169
00:49:06,720 --> 00:49:10,405
1000 个不同的 GPU 做张量并行，因为你是 Google，

1170
00:49:10,405 --> 00:49:12,780
你可能能够大幅减少这个激活值。

1171
00:49:12,780 --> 00:49:14,800
不幸的现实是，你仍然

1172
00:49:14,800 --> 00:49:20,840
会因为做这一切而承受 10 倍的 sbh 惩罚。

1173
00:49:20,840 --> 00:49:23,240
所以最后一部分通常

1174
00:49:23,240 --> 00:49:25,960
与张量并行组合使用

1175
00:49:25,960 --> 00:49:28,320
就是序列并行的概念。

1176
00:49:28,320 --> 00:49:30,360
这是一个极具误导性的名字，

1177
00:49:30,360 --> 00:49:33,507
因为通常当我说序列并行时，实际上

1178
00:49:33,507 --> 00:49:35,840
有一个叫做上下文并行的东西，实际上

1179
00:49:35,840 --> 00:49:37,740
对这个名字来说更自然。

1180
00:49:37,740 --> 00:49:40,860
但序列并行是以下这个想法。

1181
00:49:40,860 --> 00:49:43,080
所以你要做的是，我们有

1182
00:49:43,080 --> 00:49:45,920
这些剩余的项，10 sbh。

1183
00:49:45,920 --> 00:49:48,778
而这些项非常轻量。

1184
00:49:48,778 --> 00:49:49,820
它们就像层归一化。

1185
00:49:49,820 --> 00:49:52,480
它们是没有太多计算的东西。

1186
00:49:52,480 --> 00:49:55,240
而我要做的是，我会以某种方式把它们拆分。

1187
00:49:55,240 --> 00:49:57,800
我会沿着序列轴拆分它们

1188
00:49:57,800 --> 00:50:01,200
而不是沿着隐藏轴。

1189
00:50:01,200 --> 00:50:04,200
而这将会涉及

1190
00:50:04,200 --> 00:50:08,720
在每次操作之前进行全收集和归约散射，

1191
00:50:08,720 --> 00:50:10,760
我们需要这些。

1192
00:50:10,760 --> 00:50:13,475
如果你想一想，这非常让人想起 FSDP。

1193
00:50:13,475 --> 00:50:15,100
对，我们有这个东西，我们需要它。

1194
00:50:15,100 --> 00:50:18,100
我们需要注意力——抱歉，我们需要激活值在某个时刻。

1195
00:50:18,100 --> 00:50:20,300
我们需要在前向传播时计算激活值，

1196
00:50:20,300 --> 00:50:22,600
并且我们需要在反向传播时用到这些激活值

1197
00:50:22,600 --> 00:50:23,725
来求导数，对。

1198
00:50:23,725 --> 00:50:25,760
但我们现在不需要它们。

1199
00:50:25,760 --> 00:50:28,400
所以我们会通过拆分它们来存储它们

1200
00:50:28,400 --> 00:50:31,620
across the sequence axis and materializing them on demand.

1201
00:50:31,620 --> 00:50:33,800
So I would say this is conceptually very

1202
00:50:33,800 --> 00:50:37,417
similar to the FSDP style idea, where we're storing them

1203
00:50:37,417 --> 00:50:39,000
in this sharded format, and then we're

1204
00:50:39,000 --> 00:50:41,640
gathering them as we need them.

1205
00:50:41,640 --> 00:50:44,180
And then as I said before, there's a duality to this.

1206
00:50:44,180 --> 00:50:47,160
So in the forward pass, g is an all-gather. g

1207
00:50:47,160 --> 00:50:48,660
bar is a reduce-scatter.

1208
00:50:48,660 --> 00:50:51,173
In the backwards pass, this is going to be reversed.

1209
00:50:51,173 --> 00:50:52,840
Right, the all-gather and reduce-scatter

1210
00:50:52,840 --> 00:50:57,600
are going to be reversed between g and g bar.

1211
00:50:57,600 --> 00:51:00,380
So if I do this now, what do I get?

1212
00:51:00,380 --> 00:51:03,060
Well, remember, with the no parallelism setting,

1213
00:51:03,060 --> 00:51:06,940
the very first part, what I told you was you get this activation

1214
00:51:06,940 --> 00:51:07,440
memory.

1215
00:51:07,440 --> 00:51:11,120
Use 34 times sbh with an attention head component.

1216
00:51:11,120 --> 00:51:14,040
With tensor parallel, I get to divide the MLP

1217
00:51:14,040 --> 00:51:17,800
part and the attention part by t,

1218
00:51:17,800 --> 00:51:21,680
but not all these other pointwise ops that I have.

1219
00:51:21,680 --> 00:51:23,760
If I do sequence parallel, I've split these

1220
00:51:23,760 --> 00:51:25,440
up by my accelerators as well, just

1221
00:51:25,440 --> 00:51:28,040
in the sequence axis, which gives me

1222
00:51:28,040 --> 00:51:30,800
now fully linear dependence.

1223
00:51:30,800 --> 00:51:32,940
If I do activation recomputation,

1224
00:51:32,940 --> 00:51:34,560
I can even drop this second term.

1225
00:51:34,560 --> 00:51:37,280
Because remember, this is just the storage

1226
00:51:37,280 --> 00:51:38,660
that I need for the softmax.

1227
00:51:38,660 --> 00:51:40,720
So I can drop this through recomputation, which

1228
00:51:40,720 --> 00:51:44,577
gives you sbh times 34 over t.

1229
00:51:44,577 --> 00:51:49,380
This is nice to remember, because this the lower bound of what you can achieve,

1230
00:51:49,380 --> 00:51:53,327
reasonably speaking, for normal training for activation memory.

1231
00:51:53,327 --> 00:51:55,160
And so if you want to do things like compute

1232
00:51:55,160 --> 00:51:57,845
by hand whether your model will fit into a GPU,

1233
00:51:57,845 --> 00:51:59,220
this is a good thing to remember.

1234
00:51:59,220 --> 00:52:01,740
You need at least this much for activation memory.

1235
00:52:01,740 --> 00:52:04,700
You're going to need some memory for your optimizer state,

1236
00:52:04,700 --> 00:52:06,830
and you will need some memory for your parameters

1237
00:52:06,830 --> 00:52:07,580
and your gradient.

1238
00:52:07,580 --> 00:52:09,120
And once you have those things, you

1239
00:52:09,120 --> 00:52:11,720
have a rough sense of how much memory

1240
00:52:11,720 --> 00:52:14,880
you're going to need for your model.

1241
00:52:14,880 --> 00:52:16,620
OK, I'm going to stop here for a moment.

1242
00:52:16,620 --> 00:52:19,240
I have talked through both pipeline and tensor

1243
00:52:19,240 --> 00:52:21,760
parallel at this point, and so people might be a little bit

1244
00:52:21,760 --> 00:52:22,580
confused.

1245
00:52:22,580 --> 00:52:24,080
And we can talk through a few things

1246
00:52:24,080 --> 00:52:26,100
before I move on to expert parallel.

1247
00:52:30,600 --> 00:52:31,560
Good.

1248
00:52:31,560 --> 00:52:33,486
Oh, yes?

1249
00:52:33,486 --> 00:52:38,415
[INAUDIBLE] 24 over t is the MLP part.

1250
00:52:38,415 --> 00:52:44,040
The MLP part cannot be optimized through the representation?

1251
00:52:44,040 --> 00:52:46,540
You can recompute the MLP as well.

1252
00:52:46,540 --> 00:52:50,710
You can do a lot more computation than what is listed here.

1253
00:52:50,710 --> 00:52:53,510
But recomputation of what is listed here

1254
00:52:53,510 --> 00:52:55,530
is very computationally expensive,

1255
00:52:55,530 --> 00:53:01,950
like recomputation for MLP involves running the MLP again in the backward pass, which you probably do not want to do.

1256
00:53:04,880 --> 00:53:08,210
Recomputation for attention is generally cheaper, yeah.

1257
00:53:08,210 --> 00:53:09,910
Because you do it time-wise as well.

1258
00:53:09,910 --> 00:53:12,370
And also you don't want to pay the quadratic cost.

1259
00:53:12,370 --> 00:53:13,790
That's quite expensive.

1260
00:53:13,790 --> 00:53:15,310
The quadratic cost.

1261
00:53:15,310 --> 00:53:16,830
That's quite painful, yeah.

1262
00:53:19,710 --> 00:53:21,590
Cool, all right.

1263
00:53:21,590 --> 00:53:25,710
So I'm going to now talk about the last ingredient

1264
00:53:25,710 --> 00:53:28,642
in the standard parallelism toolbox, which

1265
00:53:28,642 --> 00:53:29,850
is expert parallelism, right.

1266
00:53:29,850 --> 00:53:34,310
Now that MoEs are a very standard part of the toolkit,

1267
00:53:34,310 --> 00:53:37,250
most big models are MoEs these days.

1268
00:53:37,250 --> 00:53:39,910
And that allows us to take advantage

1269
00:53:39,910 --> 00:53:43,470
of one of their big benefits, which is expert parallelism.

1270
00:53:43,470 --> 00:53:47,030
And expert parallelism, I think of it myself

1271
00:53:47,030 --> 00:53:50,310
as being something analogous to tensor parallel.

1272
00:53:50,310 --> 00:53:53,390
Right, in the sense that you're taking the MLPs,

1273
00:53:53,390 --> 00:53:55,367
right, the FFN components of your network.

1274
00:53:55,367 --> 00:53:57,450
And you're going to split them up in various ways,

1275
00:53:57,450 --> 00:54:00,657
and you'll incur a communication penalty in the same way

1276
00:54:00,657 --> 00:54:02,490
that you're maybe splitting up the matrices,

1277
00:54:02,490 --> 00:54:04,630
except here you're splitting up the MLP

1278
00:54:04,630 --> 00:54:07,870
across different devices.

1279
00:54:07,870 --> 00:54:11,230
And the systems behavior of tensor parallel is--

1280
00:54:11,230 --> 00:54:14,670
Oh, sorry, expert parallel is roughly like tensor parallel

1281
00:54:14,670 --> 00:54:18,510
as well, in the sense that this is a high bandwidth parallelism

1282
00:54:18,510 --> 00:54:21,870
primitive that also reduces activation.

1283
00:54:21,870 --> 00:54:26,310
But one important thing is that if you're doing an MoE,

1284
00:54:26,310 --> 00:54:30,030
you almost always prefer using an expert parallel sharding

1285
00:54:30,030 --> 00:54:31,970
strategy over tensor parallel.

1286
00:54:31,970 --> 00:54:36,310
And I've taken this screenshot from the parallelism guidelines

1287
00:54:36,310 --> 00:54:40,990
from Megatron, which is NVIDIA's parallelism library.

1288
00:54:40,990 --> 00:54:44,165
It's a really wonderful library and document.

1289
00:54:44,165 --> 00:54:45,790
And one of the guidelines, they say, is,

1290
00:54:45,790 --> 00:54:48,250
look, if you're going to do some of parallelism,

1291
00:54:48,250 --> 00:54:51,870
either EP or TP, you should be using EP over TP.

1292
00:54:51,870 --> 00:54:53,093
Why is that?

1293
00:54:53,093 --> 00:54:54,510
Well, this is actually a good list

1294
00:54:54,510 --> 00:54:56,710
of drawbacks for tensor parallel.

1295
00:54:56,710 --> 00:54:59,350
If you have a matrix and then you cut up the matrix too

1296
00:54:59,350 --> 00:55:02,630
finely, well, the matrices are going to start to get small

1297
00:55:02,630 --> 00:55:04,470
and your GPU utilization will suffer.

1298
00:55:04,470 --> 00:55:07,130
So you want your matmuls as big as possible,

1299
00:55:07,130 --> 00:55:10,070
and tensor parallel reduces that.

1300
00:55:10,070 --> 00:55:12,630
For MoE layers, it's a lot easier

1301
00:55:12,630 --> 00:55:15,990
to route sparse token activations

1302
00:55:15,990 --> 00:55:20,270
rather than to potentially route these dense big tensor parallel

1303
00:55:20,270 --> 00:55:22,710
matmul activations.

1304
00:55:22,710 --> 00:55:29,310
And then you can do things like route tokens exactly

1305
00:55:29,310 --> 00:55:30,610
to the places that they need.

1306
00:55:30,610 --> 00:55:32,730
And you can skip some computation, or sorry,

1307
00:55:32,730 --> 00:55:35,710
some overhead in the cases that if you're

1308
00:55:35,710 --> 00:55:37,950
going to have MoE anyway, you might

1309
00:55:37,950 --> 00:55:40,590
as well have the number of experts split up

1310
00:55:40,590 --> 00:55:43,870
over all the devices.

1311
00:55:43,870 --> 00:55:47,550
Now, this-- sorry, yeah.

1312
00:55:47,550 --> 00:55:49,810
This might make it seem like, all right, EP is great.

1313
00:55:49,810 --> 00:55:52,750
Everyone should be doing expert parallel all the time.

1314
00:55:52,750 --> 00:55:54,950
Unfortunately, expert parallel is still

1315
00:55:54,950 --> 00:55:58,010
really complicated and still very difficult.

1316
00:55:58,010 --> 00:56:00,710
I think everything that I've said after FSDP--

1317
00:56:00,710 --> 00:56:03,670
tensor parallel, pipeline parallel, systems wise

1318
00:56:03,670 --> 00:56:06,630
is quite non-trivial to get working really well.

1319
00:56:06,630 --> 00:56:09,350
And I'll just point to two different libraries

1320
00:56:09,350 --> 00:56:12,210
not to tell you the details of what happens inside of them,

1321
00:56:12,210 --> 00:56:16,110
but to tell you, this is seriously complicated business.

1322
00:56:16,110 --> 00:56:18,360
DeepSeek has a library called DPP,

1323
00:56:18,360 --> 00:56:20,110
which I think is one of my favorite things

1324
00:56:20,110 --> 00:56:23,470
from the DeepSeek V3 era, which is basically

1325
00:56:23,470 --> 00:56:26,750
their own library for doing expert parallel routing

1326
00:56:26,750 --> 00:56:31,030
and dispatching, where they're looking at really low level GPU

1327
00:56:31,030 --> 00:56:35,070
networking primitives in order to try to basically combine

1328
00:56:35,070 --> 00:56:37,910
different operations or have the hardware handle

1329
00:56:37,910 --> 00:56:40,830
certain operations in order to make MoE routing

1330
00:56:40,830 --> 00:56:43,470
as efficient as possible.

1331
00:56:43,470 --> 00:56:47,910
Similarly, NVIDIA has a library called Hybrid EP, which

1332
00:56:47,910 --> 00:56:51,990
is a similar effort of having very efficient low level

1333
00:56:51,990 --> 00:56:57,110
hardware implementations of expert parallel dispatching.

1334
00:56:57,110 --> 00:56:58,860
And just to give you the high level thing,

1335
00:56:58,860 --> 00:57:01,950
right, the reason why this stuff is hard

1336
00:57:01,950 --> 00:57:04,930
is you are doing a lot of all to all dispatching.

1337
00:57:04,930 --> 00:57:07,790
If you look at the communications pattern of MoE,

1338
00:57:07,790 --> 00:57:10,810
much like tensor parallel, every time you have a MLP,

1339
00:57:10,810 --> 00:57:12,370
you're going to have to route tokens

1340
00:57:12,370 --> 00:57:14,350
all sorts of different places, and you

1341
00:57:14,350 --> 00:57:16,710
need to do this in a very latency sensitive way.

1342
00:57:16,710 --> 00:57:19,030
Because your computation is waiting for your tokens

1343
00:57:19,030 --> 00:57:20,070
to arrive.

1344
00:57:20,070 --> 00:57:22,790
So reducing the latency of this dispatch

1345
00:57:22,790 --> 00:57:25,350
is extremely, extremely important.

1346
00:57:25,350 --> 00:57:29,270
And one final thing that is like a fun trivia about the DPP

1347
00:57:29,270 --> 00:57:31,990
library is the deep sea cloaks are

1348
00:57:31,990 --> 00:57:34,330
so intense that in order to optimize,

1349
00:57:34,330 --> 00:57:36,330
like every last bit of performance,

1350
00:57:36,330 --> 00:57:40,990
they basically looked and found undocumented ptx, which

1351
00:57:40,990 --> 00:57:45,430
is like GPU machine code or machine code like things.

1352
00:57:45,430 --> 00:57:48,190
And they found how to use these undocumented instructions

1353
00:57:48,190 --> 00:57:50,890
to further accelerate their networking communication.

1354
00:57:50,890 --> 00:57:52,550
So really, this is the level of stuff

1355
00:57:52,550 --> 00:57:56,870
that you need in order to get to the frontier of parallelism

1356
00:57:56,870 --> 00:57:59,430
efficiency.

1357
00:57:59,430 --> 00:58:03,030
Finally, up until now, I've talked about all the parallelism

1358
00:58:03,030 --> 00:58:05,110
primitives in isolation, like data parallel

1359
00:58:05,110 --> 00:58:06,630
and so on and so forth.

1360
00:58:06,630 --> 00:58:10,190
With the implicit assumption that you can just put

1361
00:58:10,190 --> 00:58:11,990
them together like LEGO blocks.

1362
00:58:11,990 --> 00:58:13,770
And this is true, for the most part,

1363
00:58:13,770 --> 00:58:16,210
for basically all the other parallelism strategies.

1364
00:58:16,210 --> 00:58:18,310
It's very easy to combine, I don't know,

1365
00:58:18,310 --> 00:58:21,390
data and tensor parallel.

1366
00:58:21,390 --> 00:58:25,790
Expert parallel has additional constraints

1367
00:58:25,790 --> 00:58:28,110
that you want to be somewhat respectful of.

1368
00:58:28,110 --> 00:58:31,590
Usually, the naive way to do data and expert

1369
00:58:31,590 --> 00:58:35,270
parallelism, which I think is in many of the old libraries,

1370
00:58:35,270 --> 00:58:39,560
is that the replicas for DP and EP are the same.

1371
00:58:39,560 --> 00:58:41,180
So in other words, let's say I have

1372
00:58:41,180 --> 00:58:43,060
a data parallelism of eight.

1373
00:58:43,060 --> 00:58:47,800
I'm going to shard my experts across those eight replicas.

1374
00:58:47,800 --> 00:58:50,540
And then my data will be split across those eight replicas

1375
00:58:50,540 --> 00:58:52,380
and routed in various ways.

1376
00:58:52,380 --> 00:58:55,780
Right, so in other words, you split your GPUs according to DP,

1377
00:58:55,780 --> 00:58:58,395
and then your EP is a subset of that split.

1378
00:58:58,395 --> 00:59:00,020
Right, and this is a very natural thing

1379
00:59:00,020 --> 00:59:02,500
to do because you're routing tokens.

1380
00:59:02,500 --> 00:59:05,180
But if you do this, there's a maximum

1381
00:59:05,180 --> 00:59:07,940
bound to how far you can parallelize with EP.

1382
00:59:07,940 --> 00:59:13,540
And it constrains how DP and EP

1383
00:59:13,540 --> 00:59:15,880
And so I think this is the final complexity.

1384
00:59:15,880 --> 00:59:18,820
I apologize that expert parallel is a little bit

1385
00:59:18,820 --> 00:59:21,500
really messy in many ways.

1386
00:59:21,500 --> 00:59:23,940
But because of these complexities,

1387
00:59:23,940 --> 00:59:27,380
there is actually this one interesting systems thing

1388
00:59:27,380 --> 00:59:30,900
that I'll tell you about before I stop talking about EP,

1389
00:59:30,900 --> 00:59:32,240
which is this following fact.

1390
00:59:32,240 --> 00:59:34,860
Remember, MoEs only change the MLPs, right.

1391
00:59:34,860 --> 00:59:36,500
They don't change the attention at all.

1392
00:59:36,500 --> 00:59:38,740
Right, so because we're cutting up

1393
00:59:38,740 --> 00:59:40,940
the MLPs, but not the attention, this

1394
00:59:40,940 --> 00:59:43,820
means that expert parallelism kind of applies unevenly

1395
00:59:43,820 --> 00:59:44,480
to the model.

1396
00:59:44,480 --> 00:59:47,160
So when I parallelize at the expert level,

1397
00:59:47,160 --> 00:59:50,180
I'm parallelizing the MLPs, but I'm not parallelizing

1398
00:59:50,180 --> 00:59:51,140
the attention, right.

1399
00:59:51,140 --> 00:59:54,580
And so in order to parallelize the attention,

1400
00:59:54,580 --> 00:59:57,040
Maybe I want to also use tensor parallel, right.

1401
00:59:57,040 --> 00:59:59,580
So I want a high tensor parallel level in order

1402
00:59:59,580 --> 01:00:01,820
to cut down my attention.

1403
01:00:01,820 --> 01:00:08,160
But this is going to then affect my MLPs.

1404
01:00:08,160 --> 01:00:10,740
Because if I have high tensor parallel and high expert

1405
01:00:10,740 --> 01:00:12,860
parallel, I've cut down my matrices

1406
01:00:12,860 --> 01:00:16,480
into really tiny pieces, and my utilization is very bad.

1407
01:00:16,480 --> 01:00:19,420
So there's this weird tradeoff where you want high tensor

1408
01:00:19,420 --> 01:00:23,260
parallel for the attention, but you want low tensor parallel

1409
01:00:23,260 --> 01:00:24,420
for the MLPs.

1410
01:00:24,420 --> 01:00:27,580
So there's this conflict.

1411
01:00:27,580 --> 01:00:28,920
So what do you do?

1412
01:00:28,920 --> 01:00:31,780
Well, in the last few years, people

1413
01:00:31,780 --> 01:00:34,380
have come up with more complicated system solutions

1414
01:00:34,380 --> 01:00:37,540
that decouple the tensor parallel

1415
01:00:37,540 --> 01:00:39,440
in the MLPs and the attention.

1416
01:00:39,440 --> 01:00:42,040
And so the attention layers get one kind of tensor parallel,

1417
01:00:42,040 --> 01:00:45,180
and the MoE layers get another kind of tensor parallel.

1418
01:00:45,180 --> 01:00:48,220
And so now you can fully decouple the parallelism.

1419
01:00:48,220 --> 01:00:51,460
And this leads to more complicated but more effective

1420
01:00:51,460 --> 01:00:55,060
combinations of EP and tensor parallel,

1421
01:00:55,060 --> 01:00:58,300
as well as data parallel.

1422
01:00:58,300 --> 01:00:59,540
Cool.

1423
01:00:59,540 --> 01:01:00,460
OK.

1424
01:01:00,460 --> 01:01:05,140
So the last thing in this space that I want to end with

1425
01:01:05,140 --> 01:01:08,000
is context parallel.

1426
01:01:08,000 --> 01:01:11,300
So context parallel or ring attention

1427
01:01:11,300 --> 01:01:13,660
is an idea of basically splitting activations

1428
01:01:13,660 --> 01:01:17,700
in a very long sequence across different accelerators.

1429
01:01:17,700 --> 01:01:21,220
And you can do things like pass the activations

1430
01:01:21,220 --> 01:01:25,980
to the device that's needed in this ring-like way following

1431
01:01:25,980 --> 01:01:29,780
the mesh topology of TPUs.

1432
01:01:29,780 --> 01:01:31,340
And so ring attention, which I think

1433
01:01:31,340 --> 01:01:33,580
was the original paper that did this,

1434
01:01:33,580 --> 01:01:35,860
showed that this worked really well on TPUs.

1435
01:01:35,860 --> 01:01:38,380
Context parallel is a standard parallelization strategy

1436
01:01:38,380 --> 01:01:39,400
that does this.

1437
01:01:39,400 --> 01:01:42,700
Both of these are used in long context extension stages

1438
01:01:42,700 --> 01:01:44,740
and in model serving.

1439
01:01:44,740 --> 01:01:47,620
I'm not going to talk about it, mainly because I think

1440
01:01:47,620 --> 01:01:49,340
it overlaps in concept to a lot of what

1441
01:01:49,340 --> 01:01:51,180
we've talked about already.

1442
01:01:51,180 --> 01:01:52,680
OK.

1443
01:01:52,680 --> 01:01:55,040
So this is the end of the second part.

1444
01:01:55,040 --> 01:01:57,840
And I think to put everything together,

1445
01:01:57,840 --> 01:02:01,263
I've made a big table of parallelization strategies,

1446
01:02:01,263 --> 01:02:03,180
including data parallel, which are the top two

1447
01:02:03,180 --> 01:02:07,300
rows and the various model parallelism strategies below them.

1448
01:02:07,300 --> 01:02:10,540
And I think I've colored in red what I subjectively

1449
01:02:10,540 --> 01:02:14,420
feel like are the drawbacks of the different methods.

1450
01:02:14,420 --> 01:02:16,740
And the reason why I've colored things in red

1451
01:02:16,740 --> 01:02:18,660
is to try to convey to you that there

1452
01:02:18,660 --> 01:02:22,360
is no one strictly dominant parallelization strategy.

1453
01:02:22,360 --> 01:02:24,300
It's all a whole bunch of tradeoffs

1454
01:02:24,300 --> 01:02:27,140
that you somehow have to manage gracefully

1455
01:02:27,140 --> 01:02:29,860
in order to get a good outcome.

1456
01:02:29,860 --> 01:02:32,160
So for example, FSDP is great.

1457
01:02:32,160 --> 01:02:34,100
It's a wonderful strategy.

1458
01:02:34,100 --> 01:02:36,040
Does not help you with activation.

1459
01:02:36,040 --> 01:02:38,380
It consumes your global batch size

1460
01:02:38,380 --> 01:02:40,460
in order to do parallelization.

1461
01:02:40,460 --> 01:02:41,780
So it has limits.

1462
01:02:41,780 --> 01:02:43,380
And to address these, maybe you want

1463
01:02:43,380 --> 01:02:45,660
to compose them with some of these other tricks

1464
01:02:45,660 --> 01:02:46,892
that you have at hand.

1465
01:02:46,892 --> 01:02:49,100
Maybe you want to do some tensor parallel to cut down

1466
01:02:49,100 --> 01:02:51,260
on activation memory.

1467
01:02:51,260 --> 01:02:53,680
It also doesn't touch global batch size.

1468
01:02:53,680 --> 01:02:56,780
But if you do this, now you need fast networking

1469
01:02:56,780 --> 01:03:00,020
and you need high bandwidth.

1470
01:03:00,020 --> 01:03:04,460
And also then to leverage your slower connections, maybe

1471
01:03:04,460 --> 01:03:07,420
you want to use some of the other parallelization strategies.

1472
01:03:07,420 --> 01:03:10,940
Right, so you see how all of these different advantages

1473
01:03:10,940 --> 01:03:13,940
means that there's a place for one of these parallelization

1474
01:03:13,940 --> 01:03:17,160
strategies in not every architecture,

1475
01:03:17,160 --> 01:03:18,858
but in many large scale architectures,

1476
01:03:18,858 --> 01:03:20,400
you use a large combination of these.

1477
01:03:20,400 --> 01:03:21,740
Yes?

1478
01:03:21,740 --> 01:03:23,540
[INAUDIBLE]

1479
01:03:23,540 --> 01:03:27,480
Are they still applicable to MoEs, or is that strictly--

1480
01:03:27,480 --> 01:03:27,980
Yeah.

1481
01:03:27,980 --> 01:03:32,420
So the question was, is pipeline and, what,

1482
01:03:32,420 --> 01:03:33,960
FSDP applicable to MoEs?

1483
01:03:33,960 --> 01:03:36,860
Yes, yes, they're absolutely applicable.

1484
01:03:36,860 --> 01:03:39,580
If anything, the standard recommendation

1485
01:03:39,580 --> 01:03:42,480
is expert parallel is like tensor parallel.

1486
01:03:42,480 --> 01:03:46,500
So you want to go up to 8 or fast connections.

1487
01:03:46,500 --> 01:03:48,620
People don't really follow that anymore.

1488
01:03:48,620 --> 01:03:52,820
But even then, FSDP or data parallel in general and pipeline

1489
01:03:52,820 --> 01:03:55,980
and tensor are used extensively in all the frontier models.

1490
01:03:55,980 --> 01:03:58,020
Yeah.

1491
01:03:58,020 --> 01:03:58,700
Good.

1492
01:03:58,700 --> 01:04:02,820
OK, and you will do this or something like this

1493
01:04:02,820 --> 01:04:04,280
as part of your assignment.

1494
01:04:04,280 --> 01:04:06,727
But one of the things that's cool about parallelism

1495
01:04:06,727 --> 01:04:09,060
and I think is nice is that you can do some math, right.

1496
01:04:09,060 --> 01:04:11,380
You can actually say, OK, how much

1497
01:04:11,380 --> 01:04:15,140
compute do I do per layer for my different sharding strategies?

1498
01:04:15,140 --> 01:04:17,620
How much communication do I do per layer

1499
01:04:17,620 --> 01:04:19,820
for my different communication strategies?

1500
01:04:19,820 --> 01:04:22,940
And if I have different devices allocated

1501
01:04:22,940 --> 01:04:25,020
to different communication strategies,

1502
01:04:25,020 --> 01:04:26,620
how do these things scale?

1503
01:04:26,620 --> 01:04:30,420
And then I can figure out for a combination of these strategies,

1504
01:04:30,420 --> 01:04:31,540
how do they scale?

1505
01:04:31,540 --> 01:04:34,560
And you can start to make plots of,

1506
01:04:34,560 --> 01:04:37,343
if I have a certain batch size and I

1507
01:04:37,343 --> 01:04:39,260
have a certain parallelization strategy-- so I

1508
01:04:39,260 --> 01:04:41,520
have three different parallelization strategies here.

1509
01:04:41,520 --> 01:04:45,080
How much am I utilizing my GPU?

1510
01:04:45,080 --> 01:04:49,060
The ratio of compute time to communications time.

1511
01:04:49,060 --> 01:04:52,020
And as long as my compute time is longer than communications

1512
01:04:52,020 --> 01:04:55,420
time, in principle, you can just hide communications

1513
01:04:55,420 --> 01:04:57,080
underneath your computation.

1514
01:04:57,080 --> 01:05:00,460
And so you can make plots like this where this dashed line

1515
01:05:00,460 --> 01:05:03,812
at the very top, that's the boundary of where

1516
01:05:03,812 --> 01:05:04,520
you're efficient.

1517
01:05:04,520 --> 01:05:08,400
Anything above that is fully utilizing your computation.

1518
01:05:08,400 --> 01:05:11,200
Anything below that is on the bad part of your roofline.

1519
01:05:11,200 --> 01:05:14,580
You're waiting for communication to do your computation.

1520
01:05:14,580 --> 01:05:16,660
One thing that's pretty interesting here, right.

1521
01:05:16,660 --> 01:05:19,420
So I think this should feel intuitive to you

1522
01:05:19,420 --> 01:05:20,840
based on what I've told you.

1523
01:05:20,840 --> 01:05:24,620
If your batch size is big enough, FSDP only is good.

1524
01:05:24,620 --> 01:05:30,890
Right, if you have a batch size of 2,000, right, per chip,

1525
01:05:30,890 --> 01:05:32,750
then how far can you go?

1526
01:05:32,750 --> 01:05:35,390
Well, you've got FSDP doing extremely well.

1527
01:05:35,390 --> 01:05:37,050
You're fully compute bound, right.

1528
01:05:37,050 --> 01:05:40,470
But the important thing is, as the batch size goes down,

1529
01:05:40,470 --> 01:05:43,745
FSDP only will hit a point where you're now communication bound.

1530
01:05:43,745 --> 01:05:44,870
Now, what do you do, right?

1531
01:05:44,870 --> 01:05:47,450
Well, what you do is now, in order

1532
01:05:47,450 --> 01:05:49,090
to keep pushing efficiencies, you

1533
01:05:49,090 --> 01:05:51,130
have to incorporate MP, in this case,

1534
01:05:51,130 --> 01:05:52,990
is tensor parallel, to the mix.

1535
01:05:52,990 --> 01:05:55,650
Right, now you can push that curve out a little bit more

1536
01:05:55,650 --> 01:05:58,810
and be compute bound even into smaller batch size regimes,

1537
01:05:58,810 --> 01:05:59,310
right.

1538
01:05:59,310 --> 01:06:02,930
And you keep adding strategies to push this curve out in order

1539
01:06:02,930 --> 01:06:05,010
to get something better.

1540
01:06:05,010 --> 01:06:07,790
And this is what people call 3D or 4D--

1541
01:06:07,790 --> 01:06:10,230
I don't think there's 5D--parallelism.

1542
01:06:10,230 --> 01:06:12,650
Which is when you put all these strategies together

1543
01:06:12,650 --> 01:06:16,730
to keep your compute units fully utilized

1544
01:06:16,730 --> 01:06:19,650
under different communication topologies.

1545
01:06:19,650 --> 01:06:21,978
There's a very simple prescription.

1546
01:06:21,978 --> 01:06:23,770
I think up until now, I've been telling you

1547
01:06:23,770 --> 01:06:25,310
things in generalities.

1548
01:06:25,310 --> 01:06:27,150
So it might feel complicated.

1549
01:06:27,150 --> 01:06:29,810
But I think actually the strategy for how you parallelize

1550
01:06:29,810 --> 01:06:31,190
is very simple, actually.

1551
01:06:31,190 --> 01:06:33,135
Which is, until your model fits into memory,

1552
01:06:33,135 --> 01:06:35,010
you're going to cut up your model by whatever

1553
01:06:35,010 --> 01:06:36,438
means necessary, right.

1554
01:06:36,438 --> 01:06:38,230
And how are you going to cut up your model?

1555
01:06:38,230 --> 01:06:40,690
You're going to use either tensor or expert parallel

1556
01:06:40,690 --> 01:06:41,990
for your fast interconnect.

1557
01:06:41,990 --> 01:06:43,740
And so if you have eight GPUs per machine,

1558
01:06:43,740 --> 01:06:46,650
you're going to use a tensor expert parallel of eight.

1559
01:06:46,650 --> 01:06:49,730
And then you're going to pipeline parallel or Zero-3,

1560
01:06:49,730 --> 01:06:53,070
which is FSDP, the rest of the way to make your model fit.

1561
01:06:53,070 --> 01:06:54,650
And once your model fits, then you're

1562
01:06:54,650 --> 01:06:56,850
going to just data parallel the rest of the way.

1563
01:06:56,850 --> 01:06:59,650
Right, then use up the rest of your GPUs with data parallel,

1564
01:06:59,650 --> 01:07:00,598
right.

1565
01:07:00,598 --> 01:07:02,390
If your batch size ends up being too small,

1566
01:07:02,390 --> 01:07:07,130
you can do gradient accumulation to get better GPU utilization.

1567
01:07:07,130 --> 01:07:08,770
Very simple strategy.

1568
01:07:08,770 --> 01:07:12,610
And this is borne out in the actual large scale

1569
01:07:12,610 --> 01:07:14,710
parallel training libraries that you might use.

1570
01:07:14,710 --> 01:07:18,610
So I've added the link here to Megatron's guide

1571
01:07:18,610 --> 01:07:21,130
to parallelizing MoEs.

1572
01:07:21,130 --> 01:07:23,750
But this idea actually applies to the dense models as well.

1573
01:07:23,750 --> 01:07:25,250
And you see exactly the thing that I

1574
01:07:25,250 --> 01:07:26,830
said, but in reverse order.

1575
01:07:26,830 --> 01:07:30,150
So guideline number one is minimize model parallelism,

1576
01:07:30,150 --> 01:07:31,410
maximize data parallelism.

1577
01:07:31,410 --> 01:07:34,730
Right, so use most of your GPUs to shard data if you can.

1578
01:07:34,730 --> 01:07:38,510
Now, if you're using GPUs, stay within NVLink.

1579
01:07:38,510 --> 01:07:41,630
So that's one machine, or so one box, right.

1580
01:07:41,630 --> 01:07:44,190
Keep expert parallel and tensor parallel within one box.

1581
01:07:44,190 --> 01:07:46,450
And then if you're going to go multi-node,

1582
01:07:46,450 --> 01:07:48,850
use pipeline parallelism to go multi-node.

1583
01:07:48,850 --> 01:07:52,568
And then if you're MoE, then prefer expert parallel.

1584
01:07:52,568 --> 01:07:54,610
And then finally, if you're doing long sequences,

1585
01:07:54,610 --> 01:07:55,770
use context parallel.

1586
01:07:55,770 --> 01:07:57,690
Right, ring attention.

1587
01:07:57,690 --> 01:08:00,210
So exactly what I've been trying to get across

1588
01:08:00,210 --> 01:08:03,850
to you through the lecture, but in practitioner

1589
01:08:03,850 --> 01:08:05,490
form that you see here.

1590
01:08:05,490 --> 01:08:08,750
OK, the final component-- actually,

1591
01:08:08,750 --> 01:08:12,810
maybe I can pause here, in case anyone's curious about 4D

1592
01:08:12,810 --> 01:08:14,170
parallelization stuff.

1593
01:08:14,170 --> 01:08:16,379
Yeah.

1594
01:08:16,379 --> 01:08:18,010
I know you said context parallel would

1595
01:08:18,010 --> 01:08:22,330
be a better name for decreasing parallel [INAUDIBLE]

1596
01:08:25,670 --> 01:08:27,410
Oh, yeah, yeah.

1597
01:08:27,410 --> 01:08:30,050
Well, I mean, sequence parallel is often

1598
01:08:30,050 --> 01:08:33,010
just you do it with tensor parallel

1599
01:08:33,010 --> 01:08:34,350
in order to reduce activations.

1600
01:08:34,350 --> 01:08:38,109
它更像是一种附加组件，用来减少激活值。

1601
01:08:38,109 --> 01:08:42,170
在很多情况下，它并不是一个独立的东西。

1602
01:08:42,170 --> 01:08:43,729
对吧？

1603
01:08:43,729 --> 01:08:47,090
如果怀疑这像是一个循环 transformer，

1604
01:08:47,090 --> 01:08:51,930
像那样，这会让事情变得更容易还是更难？

1605
01:08:51,930 --> 01:08:53,130
好的。

1606
01:08:53,130 --> 01:08:57,430
我不太确定我有多相信循环 transformer 的传说和传闻。

1607
01:08:57,430 --> 01:08:57,990
也许是这样。

1608
01:08:57,990 --> 01:08:59,210
那会很有趣。

1609
01:08:59,210 --> 01:09:04,675
但回到系统结构会如何变化这个问题，

1610
01:09:04,675 --> 01:09:05,550
这是个好问题。

1611
01:09:05,550 --> 01:09:09,330
我的意思是，我猜它可能会影响像 FSDP 这样的东西。

1612
01:09:09,330 --> 01:09:11,069
你不能真正丢弃权重。

1613
01:09:11,069 --> 01:09:12,527
不过它确实有优势，

1614
01:09:12,527 --> 01:09:14,529
那就是你不需要一直重新获取权重。

1615
01:09:14,529 --> 01:09:16,029
它在参数效率上高得多，

1616
01:09:16,029 --> 01:09:18,210
所以也许很多模型并行的东西

1617
01:09:18,210 --> 01:09:20,270
就不会那么重要了。

1618
01:09:20,270 --> 01:09:21,270
那会很有趣。

1619
01:09:21,270 --> 01:09:25,050
对于很多 FSDP 风格的东西来说，它肯定会很不一样。

1620
01:09:25,050 --> 01:09:25,550
那些东西。

1621
01:09:25,550 --> 01:09:29,450
因为 FSDP 策略真的是建立在获取权重、

1622
01:09:29,450 --> 01:09:31,350
丢弃权重、获取权重、丢弃权重之上的。

1623
01:09:31,350 --> 01:09:34,770
而在循环中，你什么都不丢弃。

1624
01:09:34,770 --> 01:09:36,529
好，OK。

1625
01:09:36,529 --> 01:09:37,290
好的。

1626
01:09:37,290 --> 01:09:41,290
所以这是一篇老论文，但它实际上可能是

1627
01:09:41,290 --> 01:09:43,450
最好的资源之一。

1628
01:09:43,450 --> 01:09:46,490
而且，网络基础知识

1629
01:09:46,490 --> 01:09:47,270
并没有改变那么多。

1630
01:09:47,270 --> 01:09:53,050
所以我认为这里的经验在今天仍然非常相关。

1631
01:09:53,050 --> 01:09:56,610
这是一篇由 NVIDIA 和以前

1632
01:09:56,610 --> 01:09:59,690
不幸的是，斯坦福的人，Matei 和 Deepak 写的论文，

1633
01:09:59,690 --> 01:10:02,610
他们做了大量大规模并行

1634
01:10:02,610 --> 01:10:05,170
训练的运行，涵盖许多不同的配置，

1635
01:10:05,170 --> 01:10:07,990
基本上是为了告诉你，当你扩展规模时，

1636
01:10:07,990 --> 01:10:10,130
你的并行化策略

1637
01:10:10,130 --> 01:10:13,970
应该如何在不同规模下变化？

1638
01:10:13,970 --> 01:10:15,610
你会看到正是这个处方，

1639
01:10:15,610 --> 01:10:18,090
我之前一直在讲的，也就是，你

1640
01:10:18,090 --> 01:10:20,890
have data parallel maxed out.

1641
01:10:20,890 --> 01:10:23,853
And then your tensor parallel increases until it hits 8,

1642
01:10:23,853 --> 01:10:26,270
and then you stop because you don't want to go any higher.

1643
01:10:26,270 --> 01:10:28,850
And then from that point on, your pipeline parallel increases

1644
01:10:28,850 --> 01:10:31,170
and increases and increases, right.

1645
01:10:31,170 --> 01:10:35,160
And with sufficiently large scale, data parallel decreases.

1646
01:10:35,160 --> 01:10:36,910
And at the very end, you have a DP of six.

1647
01:10:36,910 --> 01:10:39,650
And this is because you just need this much tensor

1648
01:10:39,650 --> 01:10:41,670
and pipeline parallel to fit the stuff.

1649
01:10:41,670 --> 01:10:47,130
Right, and you're using the rest of your budget on data parallel.

1650
01:10:47,130 --> 01:10:48,850
And if you put them all together,

1651
01:10:48,850 --> 01:10:53,050
right, even as you go to ludicrously large numbers

1652
01:10:53,050 --> 01:10:57,710
of GPUs, your utilization stays very flat and very good.

1653
01:10:57,710 --> 01:11:01,390
And this is why I think you see this gigantic data center

1654
01:11:01,390 --> 01:11:01,930
build-outs.

1655
01:11:01,930 --> 01:11:04,430
The parallelization strategies people have come up with

1656
01:11:04,430 --> 01:11:05,473
are so effective.

1657
01:11:05,473 --> 01:11:06,890
And the hardware for communication

1658
01:11:06,890 --> 01:11:10,010
is so good that even with the gigantic data centers or even

1659
01:11:10,010 --> 01:11:14,330
cross data center training, you can get really good utilization

1660
01:11:14,330 --> 01:11:17,270
across these different regimes.

1661
01:11:20,210 --> 01:11:22,640
They have really nice quantitative evidence

1662
01:11:22,640 --> 01:11:27,000
showing you, for different batch sizes for tensor parallel, what

1663
01:11:27,000 --> 01:11:28,260
is the right place to stop?

1664
01:11:28,260 --> 01:11:31,860
And it's clearly tensor parallel size of 8.

1665
01:11:31,860 --> 01:11:35,000
As you go beyond that, you're going to get into trouble.

1666
01:11:35,000 --> 01:11:37,480
And pipeline parallel, you also need big batch sizes

1667
01:11:37,480 --> 01:11:41,140
in order to be able to utilize the machines effectively.

1668
01:11:41,140 --> 01:11:43,460
That's why there's a blue to orange gap.

1669
01:11:43,460 --> 01:11:44,700
There's pipeline parallel.

1670
01:11:44,700 --> 01:11:47,360
And the larger the pipeline parallel, the larger the gap

1671
01:11:47,360 --> 01:11:51,560
between orange and blue.

1672
01:11:51,560 --> 01:11:54,200
And then I think another thing that maybe you have already

1673
01:11:54,200 --> 01:11:58,080
internalized from flash attention but maybe not is maybe

1674
01:11:58,080 --> 01:12:00,740
you should do a bunch of activation recomputation.

1675
01:12:00,740 --> 01:12:04,020
Because if you do activation recomputation very cleverly,

1676
01:12:04,020 --> 01:12:05,620
then you can get a bigger batch size,

1677
01:12:05,620 --> 01:12:07,560
and the bigger batch size then allows

1678
01:12:07,560 --> 01:12:09,320
you to get better utilization.

1679
01:12:09,320 --> 01:12:12,320
This is a, initially, I think, very counterintuitive

1680
01:12:12,320 --> 01:12:14,400
observation that actually you should

1681
01:12:14,400 --> 01:12:17,120
do more computation via recomputation

1682
01:12:17,120 --> 01:12:21,200
in order to end up getting better utilization,

1683
01:12:21,200 --> 01:12:22,900
because it allows you to save memory,

1684
01:12:22,900 --> 01:12:26,200
and memory can be turned into batch size.

1685
01:12:26,200 --> 01:12:27,160
OK.

1686
01:12:27,160 --> 01:12:30,800
So that was a more quantitative view

1687
01:12:30,800 --> 01:12:33,745
of how to pick parallelism strategies.

1688
01:12:33,745 --> 01:12:35,120
The last thing I want to end with

1689
01:12:35,120 --> 01:12:39,800
is just to look at just a very few training runs.

1690
01:12:39,800 --> 01:12:41,520
And looking at the training runs, maybe

1691
01:12:41,520 --> 01:12:44,520
we can get a sense of how modern parallelization is

1692
01:12:44,520 --> 01:12:46,720
used in practice and how it's evolving.

1693
01:12:46,720 --> 01:12:51,760
So Dolma by AI2 was a 7B open source model.

1694
01:12:51,760 --> 01:12:54,700
Sorry, Olmo, which was trained on the Dolma data set.

1695
01:12:54,700 --> 01:12:57,760
And this isn't the Dolma paper for the details.

1696
01:12:57,760 --> 01:13:02,800
Was a 7B model trained fully with FSDP across a whole bunch

1697
01:13:02,800 --> 01:13:03,660
of accelerators.

1698
01:13:03,660 --> 01:13:05,000
I forget exactly how many.

1699
01:13:05,000 --> 01:13:09,720
But this is one example of FSDP scales

1700
01:13:09,720 --> 01:13:12,400
surprisingly well, even to many, many GPUs.

1701
01:13:12,400 --> 01:13:14,680
As long as you're training a small model,

1702
01:13:14,680 --> 01:13:18,180
FSDP is actually a pretty good strategy for parallelization.

1703
01:13:18,180 --> 01:13:23,680
I think many 7B-ish models are trained purely with FSDP.

1704
01:13:23,680 --> 01:13:27,100
DeepSeek, because of their systems skills,

1705
01:13:27,100 --> 01:13:31,560
does do some pretty exotic and useful stuff.

1706
01:13:31,560 --> 01:13:35,320
So DeepSeek V1 was trained just with data

1707
01:13:35,320 --> 01:13:39,120
parallel ZeRO stage 1 with tensor, sequence and pipeline

1708
01:13:39,120 --> 01:13:41,480
parallel, as I talked to you before.

1709
01:13:41,480 --> 01:13:45,860
DeepSeek V3, that's a MoE model, if you remember my MoE lecture.

1710
01:13:45,860 --> 01:13:48,700
So they have not only pipeline parallel,

1711
01:13:48,700 --> 01:13:50,577
but they also have expert parallel instead

1712
01:13:50,577 --> 01:13:51,660
of tensor parallel, right.

1713
01:13:51,660 --> 01:13:54,760
And their expert parallel actually is a little bit exotic.

1714
01:13:54,760 --> 01:13:56,780
Because they have 64 way parallelism.

1715
01:13:56,780 --> 01:13:59,360
So they group eight different machines together, and that's

1716
01:13:59,360 --> 01:14:02,160
their expert parallel domain.

1717
01:14:02,160 --> 01:14:05,020
And to enable this large expert parallel,

1718
01:14:05,020 --> 01:14:06,600
they basically use the same tricks

1719
01:14:06,600 --> 01:14:10,000
that they have from their pipeline parallel

1720
01:14:10,000 --> 01:14:12,240
pipelining in order to try to make sure

1721
01:14:12,240 --> 01:14:16,580
that expert parallel doesn't have low utilization periods.

1722
01:14:16,580 --> 01:14:18,880
So this is actually quite a complicated trick

1723
01:14:18,880 --> 01:14:23,880
that they do to get these very large expert parallel splits.

1724
01:14:23,880 --> 01:14:28,040
Another example, Yi, from Chinese open weights training

1725
01:14:28,040 --> 01:14:28,800
setting.

1726
01:14:28,800 --> 01:14:31,800
They use, once again, ZeRO stage 1 and tensor and pipeline

1727
01:14:31,800 --> 01:14:32,480
parallel.

1728
01:14:32,480 --> 01:14:34,260
Right, this very much makes sense.

1729
01:14:34,260 --> 01:14:36,400
It's the classic data parallel, tensor parallel,

1730
01:14:36,400 --> 01:14:38,520
pipeline parallel combo.

1731
01:14:38,520 --> 01:14:40,940
And once again, once you go to MoEs,

1732
01:14:40,940 --> 01:14:43,700
you replace the tensor parallelism with expert parallelism.

1733
01:14:43,700 --> 01:14:46,040
Right, they serve similar goals.

1734
01:14:46,040 --> 01:14:50,520
But expert parallelism is just a little bit more efficient.

1735
01:14:50,520 --> 01:14:53,500
On the other extreme of a really big dense model,

1736
01:14:53,500 --> 01:14:54,960
you have Llama3.

1737
01:14:54,960 --> 01:14:57,080
The Llama3 report, I liked quite a bit,

1738
01:14:57,080 --> 01:14:59,560
because they're one of the few reports that

1739
01:14:59,560 --> 01:15:03,100
have literally a full breakdown of their parallelism strategy.

1740
01:15:03,100 --> 01:15:05,800
Not just like in the abstract, but across all

1741
01:15:05,800 --> 01:15:06,780
the different phases.

1742
01:15:06,780 --> 01:15:09,280
So the cool thing that you see is

1743
01:15:09,280 --> 01:15:12,100
they have a pre pre-training stage, a little warm-up

1744
01:15:12,100 --> 01:15:13,100
phase at the very start.

1745
01:15:13,100 --> 01:15:14,560
You can ignore that first row.

1746
01:15:14,560 --> 01:15:17,120
And then they have their main pre-training phase

1747
01:15:17,120 --> 01:15:18,800
in the middle row here.

1748
01:15:18,800 --> 01:15:21,140
And this is a very standard parallelization strategy.

1749
01:15:21,140 --> 01:15:25,800
Tensor parallel of 8, one context parallel, 16 pipeline

1750
01:15:25,800 --> 01:15:28,640
parallel, and 128 data parallel.

1751
01:15:28,640 --> 01:15:30,680
Big data parallel splits.

1752
01:15:30,680 --> 01:15:33,400
And then at the very end, because they want a long context

1753
01:15:33,400 --> 01:15:35,140
model, they do long context extension.

1754
01:15:35,140 --> 01:15:37,740
And for this, they crank up the context parallel,

1755
01:15:37,740 --> 01:15:38,940
lower the data parallel.

1756
01:15:38,940 --> 01:15:41,960
And that's how they parallelize this very memory hungry stage

1757
01:15:41,960 --> 01:15:43,840
for long context extension.

1758
01:15:43,840 --> 01:15:45,400
And notice, we're in tensor parallel,

1759
01:15:45,400 --> 01:15:46,860
not expert parallel land.

1760
01:15:46,860 --> 01:15:50,140
Because Llama3 405B is a gigantic dense model.

1761
01:15:50,140 --> 01:15:53,880
It's not a MoE model.

1762
01:15:53,880 --> 01:15:57,060
And as a side note, right, one thing that I don't talk about,

1763
01:15:57,060 --> 01:15:59,800
but if you, for example, become an infra engineer at one

1764
01:15:59,800 --> 01:16:05,160
of these companies, you'll notice is GPUs fail all the time.

1765
01:16:05,160 --> 01:16:12,060
Apparently during Llama3 405B training, GPUs failed 148 times.

1766
01:16:12,060 --> 01:16:15,980
And so you need lots of not just fast parallelism,

1767
01:16:15,980 --> 01:16:17,560
you need redundancy in order to be

1768
01:16:17,560 --> 01:16:19,760
able to deal with all these kind of horrible things

1769
01:16:19,760 --> 01:16:21,135
that can happen to your training.

1770
01:16:21,135 --> 01:16:24,520
So there's a distributed systems challenge as well.

1771
01:16:24,520 --> 01:16:28,960
Gemma 2, which is a Google open source model,

1772
01:16:28,960 --> 01:16:34,160
has FSDP plus tensor parallel and sequence parallel

1773
01:16:34,160 --> 01:16:36,140
and uses these two strategies only.

1774
01:16:36,140 --> 01:16:39,240
And the reason why I brought up Gemma 2

1775
01:16:39,240 --> 01:16:42,680
is, as far as I understand, this is basically a realization

1776
01:16:42,680 --> 01:16:46,080
of the Google claim that for TPUs, really

1777
01:16:46,080 --> 01:16:47,580
you don't need to do pipelines.

1778
01:16:47,580 --> 01:16:49,600
You just take a really big toroidal mesh

1779
01:16:49,600 --> 01:16:52,560
and you tensor parallel over that big mesh network

1780
01:16:52,560 --> 01:16:54,360
that you have.

1781
01:16:54,360 --> 01:16:56,960
Unclear whether this can scale out forever.

1782
01:16:56,960 --> 01:16:59,700
That has always been a little bit less clear to me.

1783
01:16:59,700 --> 01:17:04,400
But at least the Gemma scales, this is definitely the case.

1784
01:17:04,400 --> 01:17:07,200
Finally, I took a look at some of the models that were trained

1785
01:17:07,200 --> 01:17:11,480
this year, like Mistral 8x22B, and during my investigation,

1786
01:17:11,480 --> 01:17:13,790
one of the things that I discovered was actually

1787
01:17:13,790 --> 01:17:16,390
if you're interested in model training and parallelism

1788
01:17:16,390 --> 01:17:21,230
configurations in the wild, NVIDIA has a repository called

1789
01:17:21,230 --> 01:17:25,590
Megatron Bridge, where they release a lot of recommended

1790
01:17:25,590 --> 01:17:28,070
training configurations for a lot of different model sizes

1791
01:17:28,070 --> 01:17:29,790
and model settings.

1792
01:17:29,790 --> 01:17:32,790
And you can take a look at what they end up saying is the right

1793
01:17:32,790 --> 01:17:39,610
model configuration for an 8x22B MoE or DeepSeek V3 modeling.

1794
01:17:39,610 --> 01:17:42,690
And so for Mixtral, they do expert parallel of 8,

1795
01:17:42,690 --> 01:17:45,810
pipeline parallel of 4, and an additional tensor parallel of 4.

1796
01:17:45,810 --> 01:17:47,450
This one for the attention layers.

1797
01:17:47,450 --> 01:17:49,750
And you see this follows the prescription

1798
01:17:49,750 --> 01:17:54,270
of, keep your expert parallel roughly around eight.

1799
01:17:54,270 --> 01:17:58,012
Nemotron 3 Super follows the DeepSeek V3 model,

1800
01:17:58,012 --> 01:18:00,470
has a bunch of expert parallel as well as context parallel,

1801
01:18:00,470 --> 01:18:04,350
because they were doing extensions, at least in this article.

1802
01:18:04,350 --> 01:18:07,150
And then the last one that I want to talk about

1803
01:18:07,150 --> 01:18:10,230
is Qwen 3, which follows the DeepSeek

1804
01:18:10,230 --> 01:18:15,590
recipe, which is a fairly large amount of expert parallel 32.

1805
01:18:15,590 --> 01:18:19,390
And they have eight pipeline parallel and two tensor parallel

1806
01:18:19,390 --> 01:18:23,790
to split up things like the attention matrices.

1807
01:18:23,790 --> 01:18:27,092
It is actually interesting to see additionally

1808
01:18:27,092 --> 01:18:28,550
the different kinds of benchmarking

1809
01:18:28,550 --> 01:18:32,050
that NVIDIA folks have done for parallelizing different models.

1810
01:18:32,050 --> 01:18:34,610
And you see, I won't go into details of what this is.

1811
01:18:34,610 --> 01:18:36,650
This is in the NVIDIA bridge repo,

1812
01:18:36,650 --> 01:18:38,190
if you want to take a look.

1813
01:18:38,190 --> 01:18:40,390
There's all sorts of different configurations

1814
01:18:40,390 --> 01:18:42,870
that you can pick even within tensor parallel that

1815
01:18:42,870 --> 01:18:45,930
can significantly affect the performance of these.

1816
01:18:45,930 --> 01:18:49,950
So the systems integration is quite significant.

1817
01:18:49,950 --> 01:18:53,270
So to put everything together, I decided maybe

1818
01:18:53,270 --> 01:18:55,630
I would build an overview.

1819
01:18:55,630 --> 01:18:59,150
Really, the thing that's common to this is as much as you can,

1820
01:18:59,150 --> 01:19:01,010
all the models use data parallel.

1821
01:19:01,010 --> 01:19:03,390
They maximize the data parallel domains to the extent

1822
01:19:03,390 --> 01:19:04,830
that they can.

1823
01:19:04,830 --> 01:19:08,030
Tensor parallel almost always remains below eight.

1824
01:19:08,030 --> 01:19:10,290
And expert parallel can sometimes be big now.

1825
01:19:10,290 --> 01:19:13,310
And I think that's partially because of DeepSeek V3 and a lot

1826
01:19:13,310 --> 01:19:16,710
of the infra that they built for large scale expert parallel

1827
01:19:16,710 --> 01:19:18,830
training.

1828
01:19:18,830 --> 01:19:19,830
Good.

1829
01:19:19,830 --> 01:19:20,710
OK.

1830
01:19:20,710 --> 01:19:23,270
So to put everything together, right.

1831
01:19:23,270 --> 01:19:26,030
The highest level point, right, of this lecture

1832
01:19:26,030 --> 01:19:28,590
is we need to think about multi-GPU,

1833
01:19:28,590 --> 01:19:31,750
multi-node, maybe even multi-data center parallelism.

1834
01:19:31,750 --> 01:19:34,090
And to think about efficiency in that regime,

1835
01:19:34,090 --> 01:19:37,650
we need to really bring to bear all the approaches that we have.

1836
01:19:37,650 --> 01:19:39,890
Because you've got fast links, you've got slow links,

1837
01:19:39,890 --> 01:19:43,390
you've got techniques that make use of batch sizes and all

1838
01:19:43,390 --> 01:19:45,470
these different resources that you all want

1839
01:19:45,470 --> 01:19:47,550
to consume as much as possible.

1840
01:19:47,550 --> 01:19:49,670
But given all of these, it turns out

1841
01:19:49,670 --> 01:19:51,950
that there are fairly simple rules of thumb

1842
01:19:51,950 --> 01:19:54,550
for combining all these different forms of parallelism

1843
01:19:54,550 --> 01:19:58,070
to get in the end what is effectively

1844
01:19:58,070 --> 01:20:01,050
full utilization of your compute hardware.

1845
01:20:01,050 --> 01:20:01,550
Great.

1846
01:20:01,550 --> 01:20:05,220
And next week, I think we're talking about scaling laws.
